window.DUX_SEARCH = [{"path": "00-evidence-and-judgment.html", "title": "Evidence and judgment", "kind": "synthesis", "topic": "evidence", "text": "# evidence and judgment\n\ndux asks how people can learn about ai, learn with it, and retain the freedom and capacity to learn without it. an ai-generated answer may be useful or wrong; a fluent answer alone tells us little about whether its reader has learned. the purpose of education can include curiosity, understanding, relationships, participation and independence, not merely a finished answer.\n\n## what the evidence shows\n\nin a turkish high-school mathematics experiment, a gpt-4 interface improved assisted practice scores yet the same group scored lower on a subsequent unassisted exam than the control group. a teacher-informed tutor largely mitigated that deficit, without a detectable positive exam effect. the study concerns one school, four short sessions, and specific tools; it does not show all ai use impairs learning. [s01](07-source-records.md#s01)\n\na harvard undergraduate physics crossover experiment found better immediate post-test scores after a specifically designed ai tutor than after its active-learning class. its sample, course, lessons, setting and design matter; the results cannot establish that ai generally outperforms teachers or that advantages persist. [s08](07-source-records.md#s08)\n\na nigerian pilot found higher assessment scores for an after-school program combining ai access, teacher support and structured content against ordinary schooling. differential assessment completion and the absence of an equal-time human tutoring comparison limit attribution to ai alone. [s09](07-source-records.md#s09)\n\n## how to read the differences\n\nthese studies ask different questions. the turkish study's subsequent exam was immediately after practice; the physics study compared two modes and topics; the nigerian pilot compared an entire supplemental program with regular schooling. none establishes a universal ranking. a helpful research question is: which learners, doing what work, with what support, compared to what alternative, and measured when?\n\nthe unesco and unicef materials in this library are institutional guidance. their recommendations are useful for designing learning and safeguards, but issuing a framework is not a test of its effectiveness. we label dux suggestions as proposals, not validated interventions. [s02](07-source-records.md#s02) [s03](07-source-records.md#s03) [s05](07-source-records.md#s05)\n\n## where to start\n\nexplore [ai literacy](01-ai-literacy.md), [teaching](02-teaching-and-implementation.md), [independence](03-learning-and-independence.md), or [children](04-children-and-safeguards.md). the [source records](07-source-records.md) preserve access limitations and give direct links. the [method and questions](06-method-and-questions.md) say what remains uncertain.\n", "number": "00"}, {"path": "01-ai-literacy.html", "title": "AI literacy", "kind": "synthesis", "topic": "literacy", "text": "# ai literacy\n\nai literacy includes the ability to ask what a system is for, how its output can be checked, whose interests it serves, and who remains accountable. it is broader than prompt writing. unesco’s student framework describes 12 competencies across human-centred mindset, ethics, techniques and applications, and system design, at understand, apply and create levels. it is a framework for curriculum design, not a measured effect of a lesson. [s02](07-source-records.md#s02)\n\n## understanding and questioning\n\nan age-appropriate lesson might compare a generated answer with a reliable source, inspect a plausible error, or discuss how training data and design choices shape outputs. learners can ask who created the system, what information is missing, whether an answer can be checked, and when the task should remain human-led. these are dux teaching proposals; their effectiveness needs evaluation.\n\n## appropriate reliance\n\na small randomized estonian study asked 36 students aged 18–19 to judge fixed screenshots of answers to eight puzzles. a short educational text did not significantly reduce acceptance of incorrect recommendations, and the intervention group rejected more correct recommendations. the experiment did not involve live prompting; its sample and artificial task limit generalization. a non-significant result is not proof of no effect. [s07](07-source-records.md#s07)\n\nthis suggests testing what learners actually do, not only asking whether they remember a warning. compare independently checked work, explanations of reasoning and the ability to identify uncertainty. a learner should be able to accept a justified answer as well as reject an erroneous one. this is a dux inference, not a proven curriculum.\n\n## beyond one age group\n\nschool frameworks need adaptation for adults, language learners, disabled learners, and people using assistive technology. the sources here do not establish which sequence or classroom activity works best for each population. accessibility and choice should be designed with the people affected, then evaluated rather than assumed.\n", "number": "01"}, {"path": "02-teaching-and-implementation.html", "title": "Teaching and implementation", "kind": "synthesis", "topic": "teaching", "text": "# teaching and implementation\n\nthe decision to use a tool starts with a learning goal and the people in the room. unesco’s teacher framework includes human agency, ethics, ai foundations, pedagogy and professional learning. it calls for teacher judgment and meaningful human interaction. it describes competencies; it does not validate a specific product or training course. [s03](07-source-records.md#s03)\n\n## design around the learning task\n\na teacher might invite an initial attempt, then offer a hint, comparison, feedback or an alternative explanation, and finally ask a learner to explain or apply the idea. a learner using assistive technology may need a different workflow. these are design hypotheses; the research does not prove that any single sequence is best. compare learning outcomes, not just completion speed or satisfaction. [s01](07-source-records.md#s01) [s08](07-source-records.md#s08)\n\n## two positive studies, different comparisons\n\nthe harvard study compared a purpose-built, content-grounded tutor used at home with in-class active learning over two topics. the paper reports immediate post-test advantages for that tutor. the conditions differ in environment and mode as well as tool; the result does not isolate a general effect of ai or assess durable learning. [s08](07-source-records.md#s08)\n\nthe nigerian program paired structured after-school sessions, teacher support and ai tools. students who volunteered were randomized to the program or usual instruction. results favor the program on the measured assessment, but substantial and unequal endline completion limits confidence, and no equal-time alternative isolates the tool. [s09](07-source-records.md#s09)\n\n## institutional practice\n\nbefore implementation, educators can document purpose, alternatives, accessibility, privacy and review arrangements; test materials for accuracy and bias; explain the intended role of the tool; and define how concerns are reported. preserve human oversight for grading and consequential decisions. these are proposed practices informed by guidance, not a substitute for local law or a validated checklist. [s03](07-source-records.md#s03) [s04](07-source-records.md#s04)\n", "number": "02"}, {"path": "03-learning-and-independence.html", "title": "Learning and independence", "kind": "synthesis", "topic": "independence", "text": "# learning and independence\n\na finished task and a learned skill are different outcomes. in the turkish mathematics study, standard ai assistance improved practice performance but was followed by lower performance on the same session’s unassisted exam. a specially constructed tutor with teacher-provided solutions and hints had a different result. this is a warning about a specific design and outcome, not proof that ai inevitably weakens thinking. [s01](07-source-records.md#s01)\n\n## a thoughtful pause\n\nbefore using a tool, ask: what am i trying to learn? what can i attempt first? what help would move my thinking forward? how will i check an answer? what would i be able to explain to another person? a learner may choose a hint over a complete solution, work with a peer, or return to a book. these are suggestions rather than rules, and reasonable accommodations can change what independence looks like.\n\n## when to step away\n\nconsider a pause when the activity calls for independent practice, when answers cannot be checked, when the interaction becomes frustrating or distracting, or when private information would otherwise be shared. for sensitive personal concerns, speak with an appropriate trusted person. there is no evidence here for a universal time limit or an obligation to avoid ai altogether.\n\n## check the outcome, not just the impression\n\nthe turkish researchers observed a mismatch between students’ perceptions and exam outcomes. an independent explanation, later application or appropriately accessible assessment can offer more information than confidence alone. these checks should be proportionate, support learners rather than punish them, and be compared with alternatives. long-term effects remain open. [s01](07-source-records.md#s01)\n\n## further in the library\n\nfor how different kinds of help compare, see [solutions, explanations and hints](24-solutions-and-explanations.md). for what studies say about effort, confidence and over-reliance, see [effort, confidence and reliance](25-effort-confidence-reliance.md).\n", "number": "03"}, {"path": "04-children-and-safeguards.html", "title": "Children and safeguards", "kind": "synthesis", "topic": "children", "text": "# children and safeguards\n\nchildren are not a single age group. development, disability, context, family circumstances and the system itself affect what is appropriate. unesco calls for age-appropriate educational design and privacy safeguards. unicef’s 2025 guidance sets out ten child-centred requirements including safety, data protection, fairness, accountability, development and inclusion. these are policy recommendations, not proof that a particular deployment is safe. [s04](07-source-records.md#s04) [s05](07-source-records.md#s05)\n\n## practical questions for adults and institutions\n\nbefore use, check current product age requirements and applicable local rules; examine what personal information is requested, retained or shared; consider whether meaningful adult support and an accessible alternative are available; and establish a way to report harmful or misleading outputs. these are dux proposals derived from rights-based guidance. they do not replace a product assessment or legal review.\n\nteach children that generated content can be mistaken, that they can stop an interaction, and that a trusted person can help. avoid requiring sensitive personal disclosures to use an educational activity. in a classroom, educators should select and review activities appropriate to the learner and remain accountable for their use.\n\n## conversational systems and relationships\n\nunicef’s 2026 policy brief identifies distinct child-rights concerns around chatbots and companion-like systems and discusses preventive oversight. its landing page does not quantify how often harms occur. a conversational tool should not be presented as a substitute for trusted people or professional support. [s06](07-source-records.md#s06)\n\n## what remains unknown\n\nthis library does not establish a safe age, exposure duration, or proven universal safeguard. evidence for younger children, long-term outcomes, disabled children and culturally diverse settings needs particular attention. appropriate local safeguards may differ as systems and laws change.\n", "number": "04"}, {"path": "05-access-equity-adults.html", "title": "Access, equity and adult learning", "kind": "synthesis", "topic": "access", "text": "# access, equity and adult learning\n\nlearning continues outside schools: at work, at home, in community programs and throughout adulthood. a tool’s potential value depends on affordability, connectivity, language, accessibility and the time and support available to use it. a learner should have meaningful alternatives when a particular tool is inaccessible or inappropriate. these are dux design commitments, not measured impacts of a particular product. [s02](07-source-records.md#s02) [s03](07-source-records.md#s03)\n\n## access is more than availability\n\nproviding an account does not ensure a tool works with assistive technology, provides suitable explanations, respects privacy or supports a learner’s language. evaluate real workflows with the people who use them. avoid treating no-ai practice as a rigid requirement when a tool is part of an accessibility accommodation. review both benefits and risks of the actual configuration.\n\n## adult learners and transfer\n\nthe harvard physics experiment supplies evidence from an undergraduate course, not all adult education. its participants and course content may differ from workplace training, community education or independent study. the study measured immediate post-tests; it cannot establish durable transfer to other tasks. [s08](07-source-records.md#s08)\n\nthe research base collected here leaves significant gaps in older adult learners, adult basic education, disability, multilingual contexts, and low-connectivity settings. future additions should seek primary studies with explicit comparators and document missing or conflicting findings. [s07](07-source-records.md#s07) [s09](07-source-records.md#s09)\n\n## further in the library\n\nfor tutoring programmes in ghana, nigeria and peru, and what they do and do not show, see [ai tutoring in lower-income settings](26-tutors-in-lower-income-settings.md).\n", "number": "05"}, {"path": "06-method-and-questions.html", "title": "Research method and open questions", "kind": "synthesis", "topic": "evidence", "text": "# research method and open questions\n\n## scope and selection\n\nsnapshot: 12 november 2026. this is a targeted collection of primary research and official guidance about ai literacy, education, independent judgment and child safeguards. search focused on unesco, unicef, journals and study authors. it is not a systematic review: there is no exhaustive search strategy, formal screening flow or risk-of-bias meta-analysis. relevant contradictory evidence may be missing.\n\nsource notes identify the source version, design, population or non-study status, accessible material, claims and limitations. we link to primary materials instead of copying third-party articles. publisher access failure is not evidence that a document or a result does not exist. policy recommendations, product claims, experimental observations and dux design suggestions have different evidentiary status.\n\n## questions for the next research round\n\n1. what teaching approaches improve calibrated judgment over time, and for whom?\n2. when do scaffolds improve later transfer rather than performance only while a tool is available?\n3. how do results compare with equal-time teacher-led or peer learning, and what do learners value?\n4. how do accessibility tools change the meaning of independent work and assessment?\n5. what safeguards can be evaluated with children and caregivers without exposing them to avoidable harm?\n6. how do outcomes vary by language, prior knowledge, connectivity, disability, age and socioeconomic context?\n7. how should institutions detect and correct inaccuracies, privacy harms or inequitable outcomes after deployment?\n\n## maintenance\n\nrecheck volatile policies and product terms at use. record source version, date, access and correction history before changing claims. preserve material conflicts. confirm source links and exact denominators, review a representative range of evidence including adverse results, and assess accessibility before publishing. [source records](07-source-records.md)\n\n## second review round\n\non 12 november 2026, twelve sources were added (s23 to s34) on kinds of help, effort and reliance, tutoring in lower-income settings, ai-text detection, assessment design and survey evidence on use. two candidates were not added because their official texts could not be inspected: the education provisions of the eu ai act, which commentary reports were amended in 2026, and two rand reports on ai use in us schools. they remain open leads. the round did not find long-term studies of everyday ai use, or studies of young children using general-purpose chatbots on their own; both remain gaps.\n", "number": "06"}, {"path": "07-source-records.html", "title": "Annotated source records", "kind": "synthesis", "topic": "evidence", "text": "# annotated source records\n\nresearch snapshot: 12 november 2026. source access describes the material actually inspected, not an assertion that unavailable material is absent. counts refer to the units named. records s23 to s34 were added in a second review round on the same date: s23 to s28 on learning, effort and tutoring, and s29 to s34 on assessment and on how ai is used in schools. two further candidates, the eu ai act’s education provisions and two rand survey reports, were not added because their official texts could not be inspected.\n\n## evidence records\n\n<a id=\"s01\"></a>\n\n### s01 — learning performance and unassisted assessment\n\n**source:** hamsa bastani, osbert bastani, alp sungu, haosen ge, özge kabakcı and rei mariman, “generative ai without guardrails can harm learning: evidence from high school mathematics,” *pnas* 122(26), e2422633122, 25 june 2025, [doi:10.1073/pnas.2422633122](https://www.pnas.org/doi/10.1073/pnas.2422633122). the [20 august 2025 correction](https://www.pnas.org/doi/10.1073/pnas.2518204122) was inspected: it corrects osbert bastani’s affiliation after a production error; it reports no changes to findings.\n\n**access and design:** publisher article and the relevant sections of the [author-hosted manuscript and appendix](https://hamsabastani.github.io/education_llm.pdf) inspected. the publisher-hosted supplement returned http 403; equivalence to the author-hosted version was not independently established. the preregistration itself was not inspected. preregistered classroom-assigned field experiment at one turkish high school in fall 2023, grades 9–11. four 90-minute sessions; approximately 50 classes and nearly 1,000 real students, not model-generated participants. the author-hosted appendix table 3 reports 839 students in the main survey sample: 277 gpt tutor, 320 control, and 242 gpt base; its survey sample including honors contains 943 students. the author version says the main sample excludes honors classes and nonrespondents to the baseline survey. these counts should not be described as total enrollment. the publisher main table 1 reports 2,848 student-session observations per outcome, not 2,848 people. exact enrollment and class counts remain unverified. unit of assignment: classroom; student-session performance analyzed, with classroom-clustered standard errors. honors classes excluded from main sample. students worked from teacher-provided problems, with notes/textbooks alone, a gpt-4 chat interface, or a teacher-informed gpt-4 tutor including solutions and hints, then took an unassisted exam.\n\n**finding:** relative to controls, practice scores were higher with both ai variants; the standard interface group scored 17% lower on the subsequent unassisted exam relative to controls. the guarded tutor group did not show a positive exam effect relative to controls, and the negative effect was largely mitigated. these are short-term results for this setting and these specific tools; they do not establish long-term outcomes, effects on all ages, or that every guardrail works. mechanism analyses suggest copying or reduced engagement, but do not definitively isolate the mechanism. see [publisher methods and results](https://www.pnas.org/doi/10.1073/pnas.2422633122).\n\n<a id=\"s02\"></a>\n\n### s02 — student ai competencies\n\n**source:** fengchun miao, kelly shiohira and natalie lao, unesco, *ai competency framework for students* (2024), [official publication page](https://www.unesco.org/en/articles/ai-competency-framework-students?hub=66682); [full framework, doi 10.54675/jkjb9835](https://doi.org/10.54675/jkjb9835).\n\n**access and design:** official overview and parts of framework pdf inspected. normative curriculum framework, not a trial or a participant sample. twelve competencies span human-centred mindset, ethics, ai techniques and applications, and system design, across understand, apply and create. it recommends agency and critical judgment. this specifies educational goals; it does not show that any particular curriculum causes improved learning.\n\n<a id=\"s03\"></a>\n\n### s03 — educator competencies\n\n**source:** unesco, *ai competency framework for teachers* (2024), [official framework pdf](https://unesdoc.unesco.org/in/rest/annotationsvc/downloadwatermarkedattachment/attach_import_d418160e-3eb2-4d1c-8239-2744911cc721?_=391104eng.pdf&from=1&to=52).\n\n**access and design:** selected pdf sections inspected; normative professional-development framework, not a controlled outcome study. it emphasizes teacher agency, privacy, critical review, human interaction and learner control. its recommendation to test pedagogical strategies is a program-design principle, not proof of efficacy.\n\n<a id=\"s04\"></a>\n\n### s04 — age-appropriate implementation\n\n**source:** unesco, *guidance for generative ai in education and research*, 7 september 2023, [official overview](https://www.unesco.org/en/articles/guidance-generative-ai-education-and-research?hub=67098).\n\n**access and design:** official overview inspected; full guidance not fully reviewed. policy and pedagogical guidance, not a trial. it calls for privacy protections, age limits for independent conversations, and age-appropriate validation and design. specific age thresholds and current jurisdiction-specific legal requirements have not been verified here; avoid presenting any recommendation as a universal law.\n\n<a id=\"s05\"></a>\n\n### s05 — children's rights and safeguards\n\n**source:** unicef innocenti, *guidance on ai and children*, version 3.0, december 2025, [official report page](https://www.unicef.org/innocenti/reports/policy-guidance-ai-children).\n\n**access and design:** issuer's overview inspected; complete pdf not reviewed. rights-based guidance informed by consultation and existing evidence, including a twelve-country study; that number is countries, not a participant count. the overview lists safety, privacy, fairness, transparency, accountability, development, inclusion and children's ai skills among ten requirements. these are recommendations, not evidence that a particular safeguard has been validated or legal advice.\n\n<a id=\"s06\"></a>\n\n### s06 — conversational ai and children\n\n**source:** unicef, *when ai becomes a friend: child rights risks, harms, and regulatory responses to ai chatbots and companions*, june 2026, [official document page](https://www.unicef.org/documents/when-ai-becomes-friend-child-rights-risks).\n\n**access and design:** issuer overview inspected, not full policy brief. policy analysis compares approaches across six jurisdictions as of may 2026; six is a jurisdiction count, not children or experiments. it identifies risks and recommends preventive safeguards and accountability. the overview does not quantify risk or establish prevalence.\n\n<a id=\"s07\"></a>\n\n### s07 — a short literacy intervention\n\n**source:** brett puppart and jaan aru, *a brief ai literacy intervention does not significantly reduce over-reliance and increases under-reliance on chatgpt: a randomized study*, [arxiv:2503.10556v2](https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.10556v2), revised 18 september 2026; originally submitted 13 march 2025 under a different title. doi [10.48550/arxiv.2503.10556](https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2503.10556).\n\n**access and design:** selected methods, results and limitations of the 28-page [v2 pdf](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2503.10556v2) inspected, as well as the version history. preprint; peer-review or subsequent publication status not established. in october 2024 researchers invited 72 estonian schools; 10 forwarded invitations. forty-two real 12th-grade students completed the questionnaire; six were excluded for stated speed criteria, leaving 36 analyzed, ages 18–19: 19 intervention and 17 control. individuals were randomized. each saw eight selected math puzzles and fixed screenshots of model recommendations, four correct and four incorrect, and decided whether to adopt them; they did not interact live with the model. the control read a general introduction and the intervention received additional information. participant and trial are distinct units.\n\n**finding and limitations:** across participants, the reported mean proportion of incorrect recommendations adopted was 52.1%. the intervention group’s mean was 48.68% versus 55.88% for controls; the difference was not statistically significant (p = .471), which does not establish equivalence or no effect. rejection of correct advice was higher in the intervention group (36.84% versus 19.12%; p = .025). the small sample, selection, exclusion criteria, fixed screenshots and short task constrain precision and generalization; the study does not establish that ai literacy education generally fails, or what happens in ordinary iterative chatbot use.\n\n\n<a id=\"s08\"></a>\n\n### s08 — undergraduate physics tutor trial\n\n**source:** greg kestin, kelly miller, anna klales, timothy milbourne and gregorio ponti, “ai tutoring outperforms in-class active learning: an rct introducing a novel research-based design in an authentic educational setting,” *scientific reports* 15, 17458, 3 june 2025. [doi:10.1038/s41598-025-97652-6](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-97652-6).\n\n**access and design:** publisher article methods and results inspected; supplement not inspected. two-week crossover randomized experiment in one harvard undergraduate introductory physics course, fall 2023. of 233 enrolled students, 194 met inclusion criteria involving consent, participation in both modes and completed tests. peer-instruction groups of 2–3 were assigned together. the study compared an ai tutor used at home with in-class active learning on two topics; its tutor used pre-written answers and content-specific prompting. the reported post-test counts are 142 ai-condition and 174 classroom-condition lesson observations, not distinct people.\n\n**finding and limits:** the paper reports higher immediate post-test performance in the tutor condition. the environments, delivery and conditions differ together; this does not isolate ai from all other differences, measure long-term retention, establish effects in other learners or show that ai generally replaces teachers.\n\n<a id=\"s09\"></a>\n\n### s09 — nigeria after-school pilot\n\n**source:** martin elias de simone, federico hernan tiberti, maria barron rodriguez, federico manolio, wuraola mosuro and eliot jolomi dikoru, *from chalkboards to chatbots: evaluating the impact of generative ai on learning outcomes in nigeria*, world bank education global department working paper, may 2025. [working paper pdf](https://thedocs.worldbank.org/en/doc/0c08ef70ff0e1d81997f116923c0e2c7-0140022025/related/edu-wp-18-evaluating-the-impact-of-generative-ai-on-learning-outcomes-in-nigeria.pdf); [reproducibility package doi](https://doi.org/10.60572/cre7-j348).\n\n**access and design:** working paper methods and results and reproducibility-package overview inspected; data and code not independently rerun. interested first-year senior secondary students in benin city, nigeria, were individually randomized: 657 to a six-week teacher-supported after-school ai program and 671 to usual schooling. the endline assessment included 422 treatment and 337 control students, 759 total; these are people, not sessions. the program combined materials, adult support and gpt-4-based copilot. the control did not receive an equal-time alternative.\n\n**finding and limits:** the authors report a 0.31 standard-deviation improvement in a combined english, ai-knowledge and digital-skills assessment and 0.238 standard deviations in english; the relevant regression reports 654 observations, fewer than the 759 endline completers. differential attrition was significant; the paper reports sensitivity analyses, but selection remains a limitation. the bundled intervention does not establish the separate effect of ai, nor its long-term impact. the working paper’s projected earnings are projections, not observed participant outcomes.\n\n<a id=\"s10\"></a>\n\n### s10 — medical education observational study\n\n**source:** thomas thesen and soo hwan park, “a generative ai teaching assistant for personalized learning in medical education,” *npj digital medicine* 8, 627, 4 november 2025. [doi:10.1038/s41746-025-02022-1](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41746-025-02022-1).\n\n**access and design:** publisher methods, selected results and limitations inspected. observational deployment of a course-material-grounded assistant across two medical-school cohorts, not a randomized learning-outcome trial. among survey respondents, 22 of 56 in the first cohort and 23 of 87 in the second reported at least one use. the paper reports 328 conversations in thematic analysis (216 / 65.9% in its first topic category); conversations are not distinct participants, and topic codes can overlap. gpt-4o assisted thematic coding; a 15% subset received human validation. the authors did not systematically assess answer accuracy or track individual engagement patterns. findings describe usage and reported experience in this setting; they do not establish learning gains or that retrieval eliminates hallucination.\n\n<a id=\"s11\"></a>\n\n### s11 — evaluating education-technology privacy\n\n**source:** u.s. department of education, student privacy policy office, [privacy and education technology](https://studentprivacy.ed.gov/privacy-and-education-technology), webpage, publication date not established; accessed 12 november 2026.\n\n**access and design:** agency overview inspected, linked model terms and underlying legal guidance not individually reviewed. the page links resources on assessing how online services collect, use and transmit information. this is a us agency resource, not a study or a complete statement of requirements in every jurisdiction. laws and interpretations can change.\n\n<a id=\"s12\"></a>\n\n### s12 — child privacy and schools\n\n**source:** u.s. federal trade commission, [complying with coppa: frequently asked questions](https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance/resources/complying-coppa-frequently-asked-questions), section n, webpage accessed 12 november 2026.\n\n**access and design:** selected agency faq on schools inspected; not a trial. it describes limited circumstances in which a school may act for a parent in relation to educational services, and distinguishes school-benefit uses from other commercial purposes. the detailed requirements and applicable law depend on context and may change. dux does not provide legal advice or assert that this us guidance applies elsewhere.\n\n<a id=\"s13\"></a>\n\n### s13 — ai risk management framework\n\n**source:** elham tabassi, national institute of standards and technology, *artificial intelligence risk management framework (ai rmf 1.0)*, 26 january 2023, [doi:10.6028/nist.ai.100-1](https://doi.org/10.6028/nist.ai.100-1); [core functions](https://airc.nist.gov/airmf-resources/airmf/5-sec-core/).\n\n**access and design:** nist publication overview and online core inspected. voluntary, non-sector-specific governance framework, not a school efficacy study or a binding universal standard. it organizes activities around govern, map, measure and manage, and says the functions are not a checklist. nist notes a revision is in progress; check current status before use.\n\n<a id=\"s14\"></a>\n\n### s14 — web accessibility guidance\n\n**source:** world wide web consortium, *web content accessibility guidelines 2.2*, [w3c recommendation, 12 december 2024](https://www.w3.org/tr/2024/rec-wcag22-20241212/).\n\n**access and design:** recommendation overview and publication history inspected. technical accessibility standard, not an empirical evaluation of an ai tutor. w3c states its guidelines do not address every need. conformance of any dux or third-party service has not been established by inclusion here.\n\n<a id=\"s15\"></a>\n\n### s15 — education technology in context\n\n**source:** unesco global education monitoring report team, *technology in education: a tool on whose terms?*, 2023, [official publication page](https://www.unesco.org/gem-report/en/publication/technology?hub=83250).\n\n**access and design:** issuer’s overview and selected summary text inspected, not every underlying study. the report considers education technology through relevance, equity, scalability and sustainability. its broader education-technology scope must not be recast as a trial of generative ai. it advocates learning centred on people and attention to potential adverse consequences.\n\n<a id=\"s16\"></a>\n\n### s16 — teacher ai program in peru\n\n**source:** carla z. glave, carolina lopez, ezequiel molina and román andrés zárate, *not faster, but better: generative ai and teacher skill in peru 2025*, world bank dataset description, 20 july 2026, [doi:10.48529/t6x6-2y70](https://doi.org/10.48529/t6x6-2y70).\n\n**access and design:** primary data catalogue description and abstract inspected; full paper, dataset and preregistration not inspected. the record describes 1,269 sixth-grade teachers across 390 public schools in metropolitan lima, with school-network eligibility and school-level randomization. it identifies a program combining training, a copilot licence and follow-up, with further randomized message features. the catalogue abstract reports greater adoption without measured time savings on core tasks; this is a bundled program claim, not evidence on student learning or on ai software in isolation. the catalogue is a research lead: outcome measures, attrition, uncertainty and paper version require full-paper verification before stronger claims.\n\n**update, 12 november 2026:** the [october 2026 working paper](https://documents.worldbank.org/curated/en/099309210012625906/pdf/idu-c261aadb-6c32-48af-a07c-9cff5e86c716.pdf), policy research working paper 11476, doi [10.1596/1813-9450-11476](https://doi.org/10.1596/1813-9450-11476), is now accessible; selected methods, results and tables were inspected. of 390 schools, 196 (660 teachers) were assigned the package and 194 (609 teachers) control. controls retained access to publicly available ai. the authors report higher scores on prompt rubrics and better judgments of weak ai output, and no significant time reduction across seven self-reported tasks. time analysis table 3 lists 607 observations; other analyses have different denominators. the authors report no differential endline response, which does not remove all selection concerns. student learning was not established. dataset and code were not rerun; the research was funded in part by the microsoft ai economy institute. the earlier catalogue-only access note is retained as the record of the initial review.\n\n\n<a id=\"s17\"></a>\n\n### s17 — undergraduate preparation and belonging\n\n**source:** renlin wang, jian sun and shu wen, “structured generative ai preparation and classroom belonging in a six-class randomized trial,” *scientific reports*, accepted article published online 26 october 2026, [doi:10.1038/s41598-026-78039-7](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-026-78039-7).\n\n**access and design:** publisher abstract inspected; complete methods, data and supplement not independently reviewed. the publisher labels it an accepted version subject to further editing. six intact classes at one chinese university were randomized, three sections and 110 students per condition. the intervention combines ai exploration, confidence reflection, question generation and instructor responsiveness. it measures classroom belonging and related reported outcomes; the section is the assignment unit, and the number of sections is six, not 220 independent randomized units.\n\n**finding and limits:** the abstract reports a difference in belonging at week 12 under its section-level analysis, while its exact two-sided randomization tests give p = .100 for the primary contrasts. the complex intervention does not isolate ai or establish effects in other settings. this is a limited-access research lead pending full methods review, not a broad claim that ai improves belonging.\n\n\n<a id=\"s18\"></a>\n\n### s18 — oecd and european commission ai literacy framework\n\n**source:** oecd and european commission, *empowering learners for the age of ai: an ai literacy framework for primary and secondary education*, 18 june 2026, [doi:10.1787/65cd27d4-en](https://doi.org/10.1787/65cd27d4-en).\n\n**access and design:** official overview and abstract inspected; complete 64-page framework not fully reviewed. normative framework for desired learning outcomes; no participant count or intervention effect. it includes understanding, critical evaluation and ethical and creative use. it complements, rather than experimentally validates, unesco’s framework. specific activities and age progression require review of the full report.\n\n<a id=\"s19\"></a>\n\n### s19 — ai support for human tutors\n\n**source:** rose e. wang, ana t. ribeiro, carly d. robinson, susanna loeb and dora demszky, *tutor copilot: a human-ai approach for scaling real-time expertise*, [arxiv:2410.03017](https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.03017), 3 october 2024 version inspected. peer-review and subsequent version status not established.\n\n**access and design:** preprint methods and selected results inspected; preregistration and data not independently checked. two-month study in a southern us district, nine schools and grades 3–8. the district identified 1,787 students; 900 tutors were initially randomized, 450 per arm; at study launch 782 remained, 386 treatment and 396 control. the text also reports 874 full-time tutors assigned to schools, a separate figure whose relationship to the launch sample is not fully clarified here. randomization was by tutor, not student. final analysis comprises 4,136 sessions and over 550,000 chat messages, neither being a count of individuals. intervention tutors could request ai-generated suggestions; control tutors taught without it. the lead author worked part-time with the provider.\n\n**finding and limits:** the paper reports exit-ticket pass rate of 62% control versus 66% treatment, a four percentage-point intention-to-treat difference. it does not establish lasting achievement, effects in other settings, or safety of unsupervised child-facing chatbots. tutors reported some suggestions were not grade appropriate. classifier-based analysis of chat language and tutor interviews are different kinds of evidence from the exit-ticket outcome.\n\n**update, 12 november 2026:** a later [november 2025 working-paper version](https://edworkingpapers.com/sites/default/files/ai24_1054_v2.pdf) was located and selected methods and limitations inspected. it reports 900 tutors initially assigned; 783 with study sessions (388 treatment, 395 control), 1,534 students initially eligible and 1,013 attending at least one study session, in grades 3–6. these differ from the 2024 version’s figures above; do not merge versions or silently substitute counts. in the newer version students could be matched with different tutors across sessions. it reports the same direction for the session-level exit-ticket outcome; the authors report no statistically significant end-of-year math effect and caution that their tutor-randomized design cannot directly estimate a student-level treatment effect on that outcome. the later version’s different sample definitions and coding figures require version-specific citation.\n\n<a id=\"s20\"></a>\n\n### s20 — prompting instruction in introductory computer science\n\n**source:** ruiwei xiao, runlong ye, xinying hou, jessica wen, harsh kumar, michael liut and john stamper, *transforming genai policy to prompting instruction: an rct of scalable prompting interventions in a cs1 course*, [arxiv:2602.16033](https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.16033), 17 february 2026 version inspected. preprint; peer-review or later version not established.\n\n**access and design:** preprint methods and selected results inspected. at one north american university, 979 enrolled students were randomized to four instruction conditions; 431 with valid responses across all phases formed the complete-case sample (102, 112, 109 and 108 respectively). this 44% complete-case rate limits interpretation. students generated prompts assessed against the researchers’ rubric; the most active condition included gpt-4o feedback. the authors report gains in the measured prompting task and no direct final-exam differences between conditions. the relationship between prompting gains and exam scores is observational within the study and does not establish that prompting practice caused higher course performance.\n\n<a id=\"s21\"></a>\n\n### s21 — structured tutoring and modality in an online mba\n\n**source:** shihao yang, marshall van alstyne and chrysanthos dellarocas, *when ai tutors speak: evidence from a randomized field experiment*, [arxiv:2609.23958](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.23958), working draft dated 20 september 2026. preprint; peer review not established.\n\n**access and design:** methods and selected results of the draft inspected. in one online mba finance course, 86 consenting students were randomized to a structured course-grounded tutor or a holdout that could use consumer ai. fifty-one completed both study assessment waves; the paper also reports instructor gradebook outcomes for all 86. within the tutor arm, modality alternated weekly. the paper reports 32 students in the within-student voice/text comparison. students, assessment waves, weekly interactions and course examinations are distinct units.\n\n**finding and limits:** the researchers report an advantage for tutor access on their assessment and a smaller same-direction difference on the instructor’s final. attrition affects the custom-assessment analysis; the final was not itself a preregistered outcome. no general conclusion about all adults or all voice tutors follows, and a non-significant modality difference does not by itself prove equivalence. the tutor, subject, cohort and short field period bound the findings.\n\n<a id=\"s22\"></a>\n\n### s22 — human engagement support for elementary reading\n\n**source:** carly d. robinson, david gormley, ana trindade ribeiro and susanna loeb, *access is not enough: human support improves engagement with ai tutoring*, edworkingpaper 26-1451, june 2026, [doi:10.26300/pz7p-p388](https://doi.org/10.26300/pz7p-p388); [author-hosted paper](https://scale.stanford.edu/sites/default/files/ai26-1451.pdf).\n\n**access and design:** working-paper methods and selected results inspected; preregistration and underlying data not independently checked. two individually randomized trials compared an ai reading platform with the same platform plus in-person engagement support. district a assigned 84 treatment and 90 control students in grades 1–5; district b assigned 36 treatment and 145 control students in grades 1–3, 355 children total. the human role focused on engagement and troubleshooting, not direct reading instruction; scheduled intervention structures differed. the study is not a comparison of ai tutoring with human instruction or no tutoring.\n\n**finding and limits:** the paper reports roughly one additional minute per week of platform use in district a and 4.4 in district b, with low usage overall. exploratory reading achievement outcomes were not significantly different; this does not prove no effect. districts, measures and implementation varied, and the provider’s recommended usage level is a provider claim, not an established universal threshold.\n\n<a id=\"s23\"></a>\n\n### s23 — how students use a chatbot while learning to code\n\n**source:** matthias lehmann, philipp b. cornelius and fabian j. sting, *ai meets the classroom: when do large language models harm learning?*, [arxiv:2409.09047v2](https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.09047v2), revised 8 march 2025. it was first submitted on 29 august 2024 as [v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.09047v1) under the title \"ai meets the classroom: when does chatgpt harm learning?\" doi [10.48550/arxiv.2409.09047](https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2409.09047). crossref also lists an ssrn version under the v1 title, [doi:10.2139/ssrn.4941259](https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.4941259). the ssrn page refused the connection and was not inspected. preprint or working paper. as of 12 november 2026, no journal publication was found and peer-review status is not established.\n\n**access and design:** the arxiv version history and the selected methods, results, exploratory analyses and limitations of the 38-page v2 pdf were inspected. the pre-registrations (aspredicted links in the paper) and any data were not inspected. the paper reports three studies:\n\n- **study 1** is an observational field study of two graduate python courses at a public dutch university in spring 2023. it covers 56 and 57 students, and the analysis unit is the student–question: 6,775 possible observations, 6,594 analysed after unanswered questions were excluded. students cannot be seen using chatgpt. use is estimated from how similar each submission is to 50 regenerated chatgpt (gpt-3.5-turbo-0613) solutions, and chatgpt outages serve as an instrument.\n- **study 2** is a pre-registered, incentivized laboratory experiment at a public german university in june 2024. individual students were randomized to have or not have a chatgpt (gpt-3.5-turbo-0125) chat window during a 45-minute python learning phase. pre-test and post-test each had 20 questions in 20 minutes, with no ai access. 108 were recruited and 107 analysed after one pre-registered exclusion. most had no python experience (79%) or were beginners (17%). the lab computers blocked copy and paste, which the authors say was unintended.\n- **study 3** repeated study 2 three weeks later with copy and paste enabled: 72 enrolled, 69 analysed.\n\nexploratory analyses pool studies 2 and 3: 176 participants, 94 of them with chatbot access. they hand-code the messages participants sent as requests for solutions or for explanations. studies, students, student–questions and chatbot messages are distinct units.\n\n**finding:** neither experiment found a statistically significant effect of chatbot access on post-test learning. in study 2, adjusted p = .580. in study 3, adjusted p = .298. in study 3, access increased the number of practice questions completed during the learning phase (p = .013). the exploratory analyses report the following:\n\n- **copy and paste:** with copy and paste enabled, participants asked for more solutions but not more explanations. holding learning-phase progress constant, study 3's treated participants solved about 1.4 fewer post-test questions than study 2's.\n- **explanations:** asking for explanations was associated with higher post-test performance at a given level of progress (p = .05).\n- **prior knowledge:** participants with lower pre-test scores benefited less than those with higher scores.\n- **perceived learning:** chatbot access raised self-rated learning beyond what measured learning explains.\n\nthese findings do not establish that substitution causes poorer understanding in general:\n\n- the copy-and-paste contrast compares two sequential studies, not randomized arms within one study.\n- requests for explanations were not randomized.\n- the prior-knowledge split is a median split on the pre-test.\n\nthe field result rests on a similarity proxy for ai use in two courses at one institution. sessions were short, participants were mostly novice university students, and the model is from 2024. effects on school-age learners, other subjects, other tools or longer-term retention are not established.\n\n<a id=\"s24\"></a>\n\n### s24 — eeg study of essay writing with chatgpt, search or no tool\n\n**source:** nataliya kosmyna, eugene hauptmann, ye tong yuan, jessica situ, xian-hao liao, ashly vivian beresnitzky, iris braunstein and pattie maes, *your brain on chatgpt: accumulation of cognitive debt when using an ai assistant for essay writing task*, [arxiv:2506.08872v2](https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.08872v2), revised 31 december 2025. the original v1 was submitted on 10 june 2025. doi [10.48550/arxiv.2506.08872](https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2506.08872). the pdf footer reads \"preprint, under review.\" as of 12 november 2026, no peer-reviewed publication was found, and peer-review status is not established. a [comment by miloš stanković, ella hirche, sarah kollatzsch and julia nadine doetsch, arxiv:2601.00856](https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.00856) (29 december 2025, itself a preprint) was also located. only its abstract was inspected.\n\n**access and design:** the arxiv version history was inspected, along with selected sections of the 216-page v2 pdf: abstract, summary table, participants, protocol, session 1 and session 4 interview results, the eeg statistics description and limitations. the full eeg, nlp and appendix analyses were not reviewed. data and code were not inspected. it was not established what changed between v1 and v2.\n\n- **sample:** 60 adults were recruited and 55 completed at least three sessions. 54 are reported, 18 per group, from five universities in the boston area. the paper reports ages 18–39 with m = 22.9 and sd = 1.69.\n- **assignment:** participants were randomly assigned to three groups, balanced for age and gender: chatgpt only, web search with ai answers suppressed, or no tools. each wrote a 20-minute essay on sat-style prompts in each of three sessions over about four months while wearing a 32-channel eeg headset.\n- **session 4:** this optional session had 18 participants who chose to return. nine from the original chatgpt group wrote without tools and nine from the original no-tool group used chatgpt. each chose from topics they had already written on. the search group had no session 4.\n- **measures:** eeg connectivity (ddtf, with rmanova and fdr correction as described), nlp analysis of the essays, scoring by two english teachers and an ai judge, and interviews after each session. the interviews asked participants to quote their own essay.\n- **units:** participants, sessions, essays and electrode-pair connections are distinct units.\n\n**finding:**\n\n- **connectivity:** the authors report the weakest eeg connectivity in the chatgpt group, intermediate in the search group and strongest in the no-tool group.\n- **quoting:** in session 1, 15 of 18 chatgpt-group participants could not provide a quote from the essay they had just written, compared with 2 of 18 in each other group. none of the 18 chatgpt-group participants gave a correct quote.\n- **ownership:** chatgpt-group participants reported lower ownership of their essays.\n\n\"cognitive debt\" is the authors' interpretive framing. the study does not measure learning or retention beyond in-session recall and quoting, and eeg connectivity is not itself a learning outcome. the sample is small and drawn from nearby institutions. session 4 is a self-selected subsample of 18, with nine per arm, writing on familiar topics. the task is a short timed essay with one chatbot. it does not establish long-term cognitive harm, effects on children, or effects of other ways of using ai. the commentary raises concerns about sample size, reproducibility, eeg methods, reporting inconsistencies and transparency. whether the authors addressed these in v2 is not established.\n\n<a id=\"s25\"></a>\n\n### s25 — knowledge workers' self-reported critical thinking with generative ai\n\n**source:** hao-ping (hank) lee, advait sarkar, lev tankelevitch, ian drosos, sean rintel, richard banks and nicholas wilson, \"the impact of generative ai on critical thinking: self-reported reductions in cognitive effort and confidence effects from a survey of knowledge workers,\" in *proceedings of the 2025 chi conference on human factors in computing systems* (chi '25, yokohama, 26 april–1 may 2025), [doi:10.1145/3706598.3713778](https://doi.org/10.1145/3706598.3713778). crossref lists online publication on 25 april 2025, pages 1–22, cc by 4.0. [author-hosted pdf](https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/lee_2025_ai_critical_thinking_survey.pdf) on the [microsoft research publication page](https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/publication/the-impact-of-generative-ai-on-critical-thinking-self-reported-reductions-in-cognitive-effort-and-confidence-effects-from-a-survey-of-knowledge-workers/).\n\n**access and design:** crossref metadata, the microsoft research page and the methods, results table, discussion and limitations of the 23-page author-hosted pdf were inspected. the pdf metadata is dated january 2025. the acm digital library page returned a bot-check page and was not inspected, so equivalence of the author pdf to the acm version of record is not established. chi is a peer-reviewed venue, but the review process for this paper was not separately verified. six of the seven authors list microsoft research affiliations and the first lists carnegie mellon university.\n\n- **design:** a cross-sectional online survey on prolific of adults who reported using generative ai at work at least once a week.\n- **sample:** 333 responses were received and 14 excluded for low quality, leaving 319 participants. of these, 159 were men and 153 women, 44.83% were aged 25–34, and the top countries of residence were the uk (37), canada (25) and the us (20).\n- **examples:** participants described 957 workplace examples, of which 936 were retained after 22 were removed. examples are not people.\n- **measures:** for each example, a yes/no item on whether critical thinking was enacted, five-point ratings of effort change for six bloom's-taxonomy activities, and confidence items.\n- **analysis:** mixed-effects regressions with a random intercept per participant and benjamini–hochberg correction across 98 predictors. model ns range from 753 to 930 examples because participants could answer n/a.\n\n**finding:** participants reported enacting critical thinking in 555 of 936 examples (59.29%). for each cognitive activity, most examples were rated \"less\" or \"much less\" effort with generative ai: 72% for knowledge, 79% comprehension, 69% application, 72% analysis, 76% synthesis and 55% evaluation. higher task-specific confidence in the ai was associated with lower reported enaction of critical thinking (p < .001) and with lower reported effort for five of six activities. higher self-confidence was associated with higher reported enaction (p = .026). qualitative coding describes a shift toward verifying, integrating and overseeing ai output.\n\nall measures are self-reports in a single survey, so the associations are not causal estimates of ai's \"impact.\" the authors note that participants sometimes conflated reduced effort in using the tool with reduced critical thinking. the sample is english-speaking, skews younger, and uses ai frequently. the study does not measure actual critical-thinking performance and does not cover students or children.\n\n<a id=\"s26\"></a>\n\n### s26 — chatbot, human expert, checklist or no support for essay revision\n\n**source:** yizhou fan, luzhen tang, huixiao le, kejie shen, shufang tan, yueying zhao, yuan shen, xinyu li and dragan gašević, \"beware of metacognitive laziness: effects of generative artificial intelligence on learning motivation, processes, and performance,\" *british journal of educational technology* 56(2), 489–530. crossref lists online publication on 10 december 2024 and the print issue as march 2025. [doi:10.1111/bjet.13544](https://doi.org/10.1111/bjet.13544). also on [arxiv:2412.09315v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.09315), submitted 12 december 2024, whose record links the journal doi.\n\n**access and design:** crossref metadata and the arxiv record were inspected, along with the methods, results, discussion and limitations of the 43-page arxiv pdf. the wiley article page returned a bot-check page and was not inspected, so equivalence of the arxiv text to the published version is not established. crossref lists no correction. this is a journal article; the peer-review details were not separately verified. the appendix and trace data were not reviewed.\n\n- **sample:** 117 university students (mean age 22.61, 70% identifying as female, 55% undergraduates) took part in a lab session between july and september 2023. all used english as a second language. the first author's affiliation is peking university. the inspected text's methods do not name the country, and the participants' first language is redacted as \"[disclosed].\"\n- **assignment:** individuals were randomized to no extra support (30), chatgpt 4.0 (35), a human writing expert (25) or writing-analytics checklist tools (27).\n- **chatbot conditions:** the chatgpt condition ran through an api, was restricted to the task topics and was limited to advice rather than generating the essay. whether one or several human experts provided support was not verified here.\n- **task:** a two-hour reading and writing task, a one-hour revision with the assigned support, and a post-test within one day.\n- **measures:** intrinsic motivation; self-regulated learning processes from logged clicks, keystrokes and page views; essay-score improvement; knowledge gain on a repeated knowledge test; and a transfer test.\n\n**finding:** essay-score improvement differed across groups (f = 4.549, p = .005). the chatgpt group improved by about two points more on the essay score than each other group (mean differences 1.97–2.20), with adjusted p from .012 to .037. knowledge-test post-scores did not differ significantly (p = .438), nor did transfer-test scores (p = .996) or intrinsic motivation. process mining showed fewer metacognitive process transitions in the chatgpt group, such as orientation and evaluation, than in the human-expert and checklist groups.\n\n\"metacognitive laziness\" is the authors' interpretation of these process patterns. they state that targeted, mature measures of it were lacking. essay-score gains measure a revised product made with support, not unassisted learning. the single task, short duration, modest group sizes and gender imbalance limit generalization. non-significant differences do not establish equivalence or absence of an effect, and long-term effects were not measured.\n\n<a id=\"s27\"></a>\n\n### s27 — chatgpt-generated versus human-authored math hints\n\n**source:** zachary a. pardos and shreya bhandari, \"chatgpt-generated help produces learning gains equivalent to human tutor-authored help on mathematics skills,\" *plos one* 19(5), e0304013, published 24 may 2024 (received 15 august 2023, accepted 3 may 2024). [doi:10.1371/journal.pone.0304013](https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0304013). participant data: [doi:10.6084/m9.figshare.23935269](https://doi.org/10.6084/m9.figshare.23935269). an earlier pilot is the authors' preprint *learning gain differences between chatgpt and human tutor generated algebra hints*, [arxiv:2302.06871](https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.06871), submitted 14 february 2023.\n\n**access and design:** the 18-page publisher pdf was inspected, covering methods, results, discussion and limitations. for the pilot, only the arxiv abstract page was inspected. the published peer-review history, the figshare data and the supplementary files were not inspected. the article is peer-reviewed.\n\n- **design:** an online 3 × 4 experiment. each participant was randomized to no hints, human tutor-authored hints or chatgpt hints, and separately to one of four topics: elementary, intermediate or college algebra, or statistics.\n- **participants:** amazon mechanical turk workers with \"master\" status and at least a high-school degree. they were adults, not school students, and their median age group was 36–40.\n- **recruitment and exclusions:** 394 were recruited and 120 excluded for not attempting or not completing their assigned topic. that left 274: 90 control, 86 human hints and 98 chatgpt hints.\n- **procedure:** a three-item pre-test, five practice problems with the assigned help, and a post-test repeating the same three items. payment was $8 for an expected 10–20 minutes.\n- **hints:** chatgpt 3.5 (the \"dec 15 version\") generated worked-solution hints. the second author checked them for a correct answer, correct work and appropriate language. problems whose hints failed were replaced, so participants saw only hints that passed. on average 24 of the problems prompted (32%) failed this check.\n- **units:** people, problems and hints are distinct units.\n\n**finding:** mean pre-to-post gains were 17.01 percentage points with chatgpt hints (pre 43.51%, post 60.52%), 11.62 with human hints and 1.85 for controls. in post-hoc tests, chatgpt hints differed significantly from control (p = .011). human hints did not differ significantly from control (p = .087), and chatgpt and human hints did not differ significantly from each other (p = .416). the authors report that pre-tests were even within each topic.\n\nthe title's \"equivalent\" rests on a non-significant difference. no formal equivalence test was found in the sections inspected, so equivalence is not established. hints were quality-screened by a person before learners saw them, so the result does not show that unchecked chatbot output is safe or effective. further limits are the three-item measure, an immediate post-test, crowdworker adults, high attrition (about 30%) and a text-only 2023 model. the authors' earlier pilot (n = 77) reported the opposite direction, favouring human hints. do not merge the two versions' findings.\n\n<a id=\"s28\"></a>\n\n### s28 — whatsapp math tutor in ghanaian schools\n\n**source:** owen henkel, hannah horne-robinson, nessie kozhakhmetova and amanda lee, *effective and scalable math support: evidence on the impact of an ai-tutor on math achievement in ghana*, [arxiv:2402.09809](https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.09809). v1 was posted 15 february 2024 and v2 on 5 may 2024. doi [10.48550/arxiv.2402.09809](https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2402.09809).\n\n- the v2 pdf carries a different title: \"effective and scalable math support: experimental evidence on the impact of an ai-math tutor in ghana.\"\n- crossref lists a chapter with that title in *artificial intelligence in education: posters and late breaking results…* (communications in computer and information science, springer, published online 2 july 2024, pp. 373–381), [doi:10.1007/978-3-031-64315-6_34](https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-64315-6_34). the chapter itself was not inspected because springer refused the connection. its relationship to v2 and its review process are not established.\n- a follow-up trial is registered as [aea rct registry 14476](https://doi.org/10.1257/rct.14476). only its crossref record was seen, and no results were found.\n\n**access and design:** the arxiv version history, the arxiv abstract page and the full v1 (9-page) and v2 (10-page) pdfs were inspected. the versions report different samples, and the arxiv abstract page still uses v1's figures:\n\n- **students described:** v1 and the arxiv abstract page say “approximately 1,000” in grades 3–9; the v2 pdf says “approximately 500”.\n- **baseline sample:** v1 reports 850, including 213 ninth-graders absent at endline; v2 reports 637 in grades 3–8 (336 treatment, 301 control).\n- **completed both tests:** v1 reports 478; v2 reports 477 (241 control, 236 treatment; its table 1 lists 237).\n- **effect size:** 0.37 on the abstract page, 0.36 in the v1 text, and 0.36 in v2.\n\ncite counts by version and do not substitute between them.\n\n- **assignment:** schools were randomized. 11 rising academies schools were assigned, 6 to control and 5 to treatment, so the effective number of assigned units is small.\n- **intervention:** for about 8 months of 2023, treatment students used rori on school-provided phones in two 30-minute study-hall sessions a week, with teachers present, in addition to regular classes.\n- **comparison:** the control had no equal-time alternative.\n- **assessment:** the same 35-item grade 3–5 numeracy and algebra assessment at baseline (february) and endline (august).\n- **analysis:** the inspected versions report student-level t-tests on growth scores. they do not describe adjustment for clustering by school.\n- **usage data and affiliations:** v1 reports that individual attendance and usage were not tracked in detail. one author lists an affiliation with rising academies, which developed rori.\n\n**finding:** mean growth was 5.13 points for treatment and 2.12 for control, a difference of 3.01 points on the 35-item test, reported as p < .001 and d = 0.36. students who completed both tests had higher baseline scores than those who did not (22.26 versus 18.53). ceiling effects affected older students.\n\nthis is a first-year result for a supervised in-school supplement, not unsupervised home use. because there was no equal-time control, extra practice time cannot be separated from ai features. the small number of randomized schools, the possible understatement of uncertainty from student-level tests, the attrition and the version inconsistencies limit confidence. the study does not establish effects on the grade-level curriculum, lasting gains, effects outside this school network, or the \"extra year of learning\" equivalence that the authors suggest.\n\n<a id=\"s29\"></a>\n\n### s29 — ai-text detectors and non-native english writers\n\n**source:** weixin liang, mert yuksekgonul, yining mao, eric wu and james zou, “gpt detectors are biased against non-native english writers,” *patterns* 4(7), 100779, july 2023, [doi:10.1016/j.patter.2023.100779](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.patter.2023.100779). author-posted version: [arxiv:2304.02819v3](https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.02819v3), 10 july 2023. earlier versions are v1 (6 april 2023) and v2 (18 april 2023).\n\n**access and design:** the arxiv abstract page, version history and full 9-page v3 pdf were inspected. the v3 abstract points to the published *patterns* article. the publisher's full text and pdf returned http 403, so the published version was not checked and its match to v3 is not established. crossref metadata for the doi was inspected. it gives volume 4, issue 7, article number 100779, issue date july 2023, publisher elsevier, and a cc by-nc-nd 4.0 licence for the version of record. it lists no correction or update. that is not proof that none exists. the publisher's article-type label (for example, research article or opinion) was not inspected. the study is a small benchmarking study of text, with no human participants. its units are essays and other texts, not writers:\n- **main comparison:** seven off-the-shelf detectors, accessed 15 march 2023, were run on 91 human-written toefl essays and 88 us eighth-grade essays. the toefl essays (year ≤ 2020) came from a chinese educational forum. the eighth-grade essays came from the hewlett foundation asap dataset.\n- **detectors:** originality.ai, quil.org, sapling, the openai detector hosted on hugging face, crossplag, gptzero and zerogpt.\n- **rewriting experiments:** the researchers used chatgpt to rewrite essays, with the “march 14 version of chatgpt 3.5” stated in the methods. the discussion section refers to a “gpt-4 intervention” for the toefl rewrite. this inconsistency is not resolved in v3.\n- **bypass experiments:** 31 usable chatgpt-3.5 college-admission essays were generated, from 70 attempts across 7 common app prompts. a separate set used 145 stanford cs224n project titles.\n- **statistics:** perplexity was measured with gpt-2 xl. comparisons used one-sided paired t-tests.\n- **code and data:** stated to be on github and zenodo. neither was rerun here.\n- **limitations stated by the authors:** small samples; most detectors used gpt-2 backbones; and only perplexity-based and supervised detectors were covered.\n\n**finding:** for the toefl essays, v3 reports an average false-positive rate of 61.22% across the seven detectors. all seven flagged 18 of 91 essays (19.78%) as ai-generated, and at least one detector flagged 89 of 91 (97.80%). the detectors were described as near-perfect on the eighth-grade essays.\n- **rewriting toefl essays:** prompting chatgpt to “enhance the word choices to sound more like that of a native speaker” cut the average false-positive rate from 61.22% to 11.77%.\n- **simplifying eighth-grade essays:** “simplifying” the word choices in the eighth-grade essays raised their average misclassification from 5.19% to 56.65%.\n- **self-editing generated text:** asking chatgpt to self-edit its own text lowered detection rates. for essays, rates fell from up to 100% to up to 13%. for abstracts, they fell from up to 68% to up to 28%.\n- **authors' explanation and caution:** they attribute the pattern to lower text perplexity. they “strongly caution” against using gpt detectors in evaluative or educational settings.\n\nwhat this does not establish:\n- that current detectors, which have changed since march 2023, show the same rates;\n- results for other languages, ages or proficiency levels;\n- that the two essay sets differ only in writers' language background (task, prompt, age and source also differ);\n- what false-positive rate any particular school would see in practice.\n\nit is evidence that detector scores alone are an unsafe basis for misconduct decisions. it is not a validated estimate of how often detectors err in current classroom use.\n\n<a id=\"s30\"></a>\n\n### s30 — the ai assessment scale and its revision\n\n**source:** mike perkins, leon furze, jasper roe and jason macvaugh, “the artificial intelligence assessment scale (aias): a framework for ethical integration of generative ai in educational assessment,” *journal of university teaching and learning practice* 21(06), 2024, [doi:10.53761/q3azde36](https://doi.org/10.53761/q3azde36); [journal page](https://open-publishing.org/journals/index.php/jutlp/article/view/810). revised version: mike perkins, jasper roe and leon furze, “reimagining the artificial intelligence assessment scale: a refined framework for educational assessment,” *journal of university teaching and learning practice* 22(7), 2025, [doi:10.53761/rrm4y757](https://doi.org/10.53761/rrm4y757); [journal page](https://open-publishing.org/journals/index.php/jutlp/article/view/1707).\n\n**access and design:** for both articles the journal landing page, full pdf and crossref metadata were inspected. the two are conceptual and normative frameworks. they report no participants, no trial and no outcome data.\n- **date and licence discrepancies, original article:** the pdf gives received 4 december 2023, revised 16 february 2024, accepted 13 march 2024 and published 30 april 2024, under cc by-nc-sa 4.0. the landing page gives received 14 april 2024 and published online 19 april 2024, under cc by-nd 4.0. crossref gives 19 april 2024. these do not agree.\n- **revised article dates:** the pdf gives submitted 15 april 2025, revised 12 august 2025, accepted 25 september 2025 and online first 27 september 2025. the landing page and crossref give published online 3 december 2025.\n- **peer review:** both carry journal editorial details and received or accepted dates. the review process itself was not inspected.\n- **crossref metadata error:** crossref swaps the given and family names of the revision's second author, and the journal's generated apa citation repeats the error (“jasper, r.”). the pdf's own citation reads “perkins, m., roe, j., & furze, l. (2025)”.\n- **declarations in the revision:** no conflicts of interest and no external funding. the authors say they used chatgpt (o3-mini-high) and claude 3.5 sonnet for ideation, some draft text and editing.\n- **earlier preprint:** an [arxiv preprint, 2412.09029](https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.09029), titled “the ai assessment scale revisited,” exists. it was not inspected and should not be cited in place of the journal version.\n\n**finding:** the original aias sets out five levels of permitted generative-ai use in an assessment, from “no ai” to “full ai”. it is meant to let educators match permitted use to learning outcomes and to make expectations clear. its own limitations section says the scale “has only recently been put into operation in a pilot cohort” and that “there is no empirical research on the scale’s effectiveness in combating academic misconduct or improving student outcomes.”\n\nthe 2025 revision keeps five levels: no ai, ai planning, ai collaboration, full ai and ai exploration. its main changes are these:\n- it replaces the traffic-light colours with a non-hierarchical design;\n- it drops the old level 3 requirement to append unassisted original work, because detection cannot verify such submissions;\n- it says level 1 should take place in supervised settings, not rely on honour systems or detectors;\n- it presents the scale as a tool for redesigning assessments, not a label. it warns that relabelling an unchanged task “level 3” does not improve validity.\n\nit sets the aias alongside the “two-lane” model and accepts critiques that rules alone are “discursive” and need structural redesign. the revision says the scale has been implemented in “hundreds of institutions” and translated into 30 languages. these are the developers' own claims. it cites a pilot (furze et al., 2024) as early evidence, which was not inspected, and says “further empirical validation is crucial.”\n\nthe aias is a design and communication framework. its effect on learning, misconduct or assessment validity has not been established by these papers, and the authors developed the scale they recommend.\n\n<a id=\"s31\"></a>\n\n### s31 — us teens' use of chatgpt for schoolwork, fall 2024\n\n**source:** olivia sidoti, eugenie park and jeffrey gottfried, pew research center, “about a quarter of u.s. teens have used chatgpt for schoolwork – double the share in 2023,” short read, 15 january 2025, [official page](https://www.pewresearch.org/short-reads/2025/01/15/about-a-quarter-of-us-teens-have-used-chatgpt-for-schoolwork-double-the-share-in-2023/); [methodology pdf](https://www.pewresearch.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/20/2025/01/sr_25.01.15_teens-chatgpt_methodology.pdf); [topline pdf](https://www.pewresearch.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/20/2025/01/sr_25.01.15_teens-chatgpt_topline.pdf).\n\n**access and design:** the short read, methodology and topline were inspected. this is a cross-sectional, self-administered web survey of 1,391 parent–teen dyads, with us teens aged 13–17, fielded 18 september to 10 october 2024.\n- **mode and panel:** run by ipsos in english and spanish on knowledgepanel, a probability-based panel recruited mainly by address-based sampling. teens were reached through their parents.\n- **response:** 3,233 panelists were invited and 1,391 qualifying dyads completed the survey. the completion rate was 51.5% and the cumulative response rate 2.7%.\n- **weighting and error:** results are weighted to us teens aged 13–17 who live with parents. the margin of error is ±3.3 percentage points for all teens. it is larger for subgroups, for example ±8.0 points for black teens (unweighted n = 247), because the survey oversampled black and hispanic respondents.\n- **ethics review:** an external irb (advarra) approved the study.\n- **question asked:** “have you ever used chatgpt to help with your schoolwork?” it went only to teens who had heard of chatgpt (unweighted n = 1,056) and was then re-based on all teens. it measures any use ever, of one named product. it does not measure how often teens use it, any other chatbots, or whether use was permitted.\n- **trend comparison:** the 2023 figures come from a survey fielded 26 september to 23 october 2023, not inspected here.\n- **measurement limits:** all measures are teen self-reports.\n\n**finding:** 26% of all us teens said they had ever used chatgpt to help with schoolwork, up from 13% in 2023. that is 34% of teens who had heard of chatgpt. the short read reports:\n- black and hispanic teens: 31% each, compared with 22% of white teens.\n- grades 11–12: 31%, compared with 20% in grades 7–8.\n- gender and household income: no difference in use.\n- acceptability, among all teens: 54% said using chatgpt to research new topics is acceptable. for solving math problems the figure was 29% (28% not acceptable), and for writing essays 18% (42% not acceptable).\n- awareness: 79% had heard of chatgpt.\n\nthese are population estimates of self-reported use. they do not show how intensively teens used it, what they produced, whether the use was misconduct, or any effect on learning. the figure is specific to chatgpt and should not be equated with use of ai chatbots in general (see s32).\n\n<a id=\"s32\"></a>\n\n### s32 — how us teens use ai chatbots, including for schoolwork, fall 2025\n\n**source:** colleen mcclain, monica anderson, olivia sidoti and william bishop, pew research center, *how teens use and view ai*, 24 february 2026, [official report page](https://www.pewresearch.org/internet/2026/02/24/how-teens-use-and-view-ai/); [report pdf](https://www.pewresearch.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/20/2026/02/pi_2026.02.24_teens-and-ai_report.pdf).\n\n**access and design:** the report page and full pdf, including methodology and topline, were inspected. a related demographic page and an earlier pew short read on teens' chatbot use were not inspected. this is a cross-sectional, self-administered web survey of 1,458 parent–teen dyads, with us teens aged 13–17, fielded 25 september to 9 october 2025.\n- **mode and panel:** ipsos knowledgepanel, in english and spanish.\n- **response:** 3,516 panelists were invited, 2,067 were eligible and 1,458 completed. the study-level response rate (aapor rr1) was 45% and the cumulative response rate 1.2%.\n- **error and ethics review:** the margin of error is ±3.3 percentage points for teens and for parents. an external irb (advarra) approved the study.\n- **question wording:** items ask about “artificial intelligence (ai) chatbots like chatgpt, copilot or character.ai”. they are not the chatgpt-specific items in s31, and the report presents no trend comparison with them.\n- **bases:** the detailed schoolwork items were asked of teens who had used chatbots for schoolwork help (unweighted n = 766). the cheating-perception item was asked of non-homeschooled teens who had heard of chatbots (unweighted n = 1,256). results are also re-based on all teens.\n- **measurement limits:** all measures are self-report. the cheating item records what teens perceive about other students.\n\n**finding:** among all teens:\n- 64% said they use ai chatbots.\n- 54% said they had ever used chatbots to get help with schoolwork.\n- 10% said they do all or almost all (3%) or most (7%) of their schoolwork with chatbot help. another 21% said some and 23% said a little.\n- for specific school tasks: 48% had used chatbots to research a topic, 43% to solve a math problem and 35% to edit something they wrote.\n- 26% rated chatbots extremely or very helpful for schoolwork.\n- 59% said students at their school use ai chatbots to cheat at least somewhat often, 14% rarely or never, and 15% were not sure. the figure was 76% among teens who had used chatbots for schoolwork.\n\nthe cheating figure is a perception of peers, not a measured rate of misconduct. these figures should not be read as a straight rise from s31's 26%: the product, wording and question flow all differ. the report does not measure learning outcomes, whether use was permitted, or the quality of help received.\n\n<a id=\"s33\"></a>\n\n### s33 — us teachers' ai use and self-estimated time savings\n\n**source:** walton family foundation and gallup, *teaching for tomorrow: unlocking six weeks a year with ai*, june 2025, [gallup-hosted report pdf](https://www.gallup.com/file/analytics/691922/walton-family-foundation-gallup-teachers-ai-report.pdf); andrea malek ash, gallup, “three in 10 teachers use ai weekly, saving six weeks a year,” 24 june 2025, [official article](https://news.gallup.com/poll/691967/three-teachers-weekly.aspx).\n\n**access and design:** the gallup article and full report pdf were inspected. the gallup-hosted and [walton-hosted](https://static.waltonfamilyfoundation.org/df/fb/eba12807470a9402d7433cc47dba/teaching-for-tomorrow-unlocking-six-weeks-a-year-with-ai-report.pdf) copies have identical extracted text. the survey ran on the web from 18 march to 11 april 2025, with 2,232 us public k-12 teachers recruited from the rand american teacher panel, a probability-based panel.\n- **weighting and error:** the sample was weighted to national teacher and school characteristics. the margin of error is ±2.5 percentage points for all teachers. subgroup unweighted counts, including weekly ai users, are not reported in the inspected text.\n- **peer review:** this is a sponsored survey report, not peer-reviewed. the walton family foundation commissioned it.\n- **how the time figure was built:** teachers who used ai for a task at least monthly said whether it saved time, made no difference or added time. those who reported a change estimated the hours per week, to the nearest half hour. net estimates per task were trimmed at the 2.5th and 97.5th percentiles, then summed across six non-overlapping tasks: worksheets, assessments, modifying materials, grading or feedback, analysing student data, and administrative work.\n- **inconsistency in the text:** the methodology defines the “ai dividend” as an average among teachers using ai at least monthly. the 5.9-hour figure is reported for teachers using ai at least weekly.\n- **measurement limits:** this is self-estimated time. there is no time-use diary, observation or comparison group.\n\n**finding:** six in ten teachers reported using an ai tool for work in 2024–25: 32% at least weekly, 28% less often and 40% not at all.\n- **time saved by task:** among teachers using ai for a given task, 60% to 84% said it saved time on that task, and 7% or fewer said it took more time.\n- **the headline figure:** teachers using ai at least weekly estimated saving 5.9 hours per week on average. weekly users estimated 5.9 hours and monthly users 2.9 hours.\n- **“six weeks a year”:** gallup multiplies 5.9 hours by 37.4 contracted weeks to get 220.66 hours, then divides by a 37.5-hour contracted week. this is an extrapolation of a self-estimated weekly figure, not measured annual time.\n- **policy and training:** only 19% of teachers said their school has an ai policy. teachers at such schools were more likely to have used ai (70% vs 60%). the report gives 2.3 vs 1.7 hours saved per week per teacher by policy status, but the inspected text does not explain the basis of that per-teacher average. it is a cross-sectional association, not evidence that policies cause savings. 68% had received no school or district ai training.\n- **quality:** majorities of task users reported higher-quality work, from 57% for grading and feedback to 74% for administrative work.\n\nthe survey does not establish actual time saved, what the time was used for (the reinvestment examples are qualitative quotes), effects on students, or the accuracy of ai-assisted grading.\n\n**update, 12 november 2026:** two later gallup releases were inspected. they use different samples and measures and should not be merged with the 2025 figures.\n- **guidance, 2026:** andrea malek ash and katherine senseman, “[most teachers receive no formal guidance on ai use](https://news.gallup.com/poll/710534/teachers-receive-no-formal-guidance.aspx),” 26 may 2026. this is from the walton–gallup study *teaching for tomorrow: closing the expectations gap*, a web survey of 2,069 us public k-12 teachers from the rand american teacher panel, fielded 9 february to 2 march 2026, margin of error ±2.5 points. it reports that 18% of teachers receive any formal administrator guidance on ai use for their work. across 10 tasks, 34% receive no guidance and 48% only informal guidance. the full report was not inspected.\n- **time for what matters, 2026:** megan brenan, “[teachers using ai say it frees up time for what matters most](https://news.gallup.com/poll/715364/teachers-using-say-frees-time-matters-most.aspx),” 14 october 2026. this surveyed 1,952 us k-12 “educators” from the probability-based gallup panel, fielded 28 july to 14 august 2026, margin of error ±2.7 points. unlike the earlier sample, it includes paraprofessionals, substitutes and other classroom staff, not only teachers. 59% said they used ai tools for work in 2025–26. among those users, 13% said ai gave them a lot more time for the parts of the job that matter most, 51% somewhat more, 29% no effect, and 7% said it cost them time. this is a different question from the 2025 hours estimate.\n\n<a id=\"s34\"></a>\n\n### s34 — us children's ai use, ages 9–17, spring 2026\n\n**source:** eleanor o’neil, kristen soltis anderson, baxter white, laura zimmermann, supreet mann and michael b. robb, *the common sense media census: ai use by tweens and teens, 2026*, common sense media, 2026, [report pdf](https://www.commonsensemedia.org/sites/default/files/research/report/2026-ai-use-by-tweens-and-teens.pdf). the exact release date was not established.\n\n**access and design:** the full 60-page pdf, including methodology and supplemental tables, was inspected. the report's landing page was not reachable. this is a cross-sectional, self-administered web survey of 1,204 us children aged 9–17, fielded 18–26 march 2026 by ssrs.\n- **panel and language:** children were reached through parents in the ssrs opinion panel, which is recruited from an address-based sample plus a prepaid-cellphone rdd sample. 1,154 children answered in english and 50 in spanish.\n- **response:** 1,421 were invited. the completion rate was 84.73% and the cumulative response rate 4.01%.\n- **weighting and error:** results are weighted to us children aged 9–17. the margin of error is ±3.8 percentage points.\n- **questionnaire:** developed with echelon insights, whose staff are co-authors, and pretested with six cognitive interviews. the median completion time was about seven minutes.\n- **funding:** the report names foundation and philanthropic funders. it was not peer-reviewed.\n- **what counted as “ai”:** the survey's definition was broad and included ai summaries in search results.\n- **measurement limits:** all measures are self-report by children, including some as young as 9.\n\n**finding:**\n- **overall use:** 86% of children said they use or interact with any ai, and 24% daily. this includes search summaries, which 75% had encountered. 67% had used ai chatbots.\n- **schoolwork, among ai users (n = 1,032):** 85% use ai for help with schoolwork or homework. 48% do so at least weekly, including 21% daily.\n- **homework difficulty:** 16% of homework users said they have difficulty starting or completing homework without ai.\n- **devices, among ai users:** 44% use ai on a school computer or tablet and 78% on their own device.\n- **school communication, all children:** 73% said their school or a teacher had communicated this school year about what they should and should not use ai for in schoolwork. 56% said the school had discussed using ai safely, and 51% had heard how to tell whether ai information is accurate.\n- **wellbeing:** heavier users more often reported loneliness. the report itself says a single survey “cannot determine” the direction of this association or rule out other factors.\n\nthe census does not measure learning, whether use was permitted, or the accuracy of what children received. because of the broad definition, “uses ai” is not the same as “uses a chatbot”.\n", "number": "07"}, {"path": "08-assessment.html", "title": "Assessment in an AI-rich setting", "kind": "synthesis", "topic": "assessment", "text": "# assessment in an ai-rich setting\n\nassessment asks what a learner understands, can do and can explain. the answer may differ when a tool is available from when it is not. the turkish mathematics study illustrates that distinction on immediate post-practice exams, but does not specify a universal assessment policy. [s01](07-source-records.md#s01)\n\n## choose the construct first\n\na learner may need to demonstrate independent arithmetic, evaluate a source, revise a draft, collaborate or use an accessibility tool. these are different constructs. an assessment that bans every aid may exclude a learner without measuring the intended ability; a task that permits automatic solutions may not show whether a foundational skill was acquired. teachers should decide what they want to learn about the learner, and design an accessible assessment accordingly. this is an educational design proposal, not a trial result.\n\n## evidence in context\n\nthe undergraduate physics study used pre- and immediate post-tests after two short lessons. the nigerian study used a paper assessment after a six-week program and had unequal follow-up completion. neither should be read as proof of long-term transfer. [s08](07-source-records.md#s08) [s09](07-source-records.md#s09)\n\n## possible approaches to evaluate\n\nuse an explanation or short conversation alongside a finished product; compare an initial attempt with a revision; ask a learner to check a deliberately flawed answer; or offer an appropriately accessible independent task. assess the approach for fairness, workload and whether it measures the stated goal. do not infer misconduct solely from a detector or an ai-like writing style; the sources here do not establish a reliable detection method.\n\n## further in the library\n\nfor evidence on ai-text detectors and on assessment scales that set permitted ai use task by task, see [detection, trust and assessment design](27-detectors-and-assessment-design.md).\n", "number": "08"}, {"path": "09-privacy-accountability.html", "title": "Privacy, data and accountability", "kind": "synthesis", "topic": "safety", "text": "# privacy, data and accountability\n\neducational tools may handle prompts, student work, usage logs, account information and sensitive context. institutions should understand the actual service and its data flows before adopting it. a model answer can also contain invented personal or factual information. adult oversight does not by itself erase these risks. [s04](07-source-records.md#s04) [s05](07-source-records.md#s05)\n\n## questions to ask a provider\n\n- what information is necessary for the learning activity, and what can be omitted?\n- who can access it, where is it stored, and how long is it retained?\n- can it be used for advertising, training or other purposes? what choices actually exist?\n- how can a person correct or remove information, report an incident, or leave the service?\n- what happens if the service changes its terms or is unavailable?\n\nthese are due-diligence questions, not proof that any answer is sufficient. the us department of education links a model terms checklist for evaluating collection, use and transmission. its page is a us resource and does not settle legal requirements for every organization. [s11](07-source-records.md#s11)\n\n## children and school agreements\n\nthe ftc’s school faq describes specific conditions for school consent in the us and distinguishes school-benefit uses from other commercial purposes. whether and how an institution may use a service depends on current law, age, service design and agreements. seek appropriate local advice; do not treat this overview as a legal checklist. [s12](07-source-records.md#s12)\n\n## human responsibility\n\nestablish who approves a tool, who reviews its output, how learners can contest a decision, and how to suspend a use that is causing harm. human review should have the time, authority and information to change an outcome. these are proposed governance practices grounded in institutional guidance, not verified results of a particular program. [s03](07-source-records.md#s03) [s05](07-source-records.md#s05)\n", "number": "09"}, {"path": "10-tool-evaluation.html", "title": "Evaluating an educational AI tool", "kind": "synthesis", "topic": "teaching", "text": "# evaluating an educational ai tool\n\nno single feature label proves a tool is safe, accurate or educationally effective. evaluate a particular product, version and use case against a specific learning goal and a realistic alternative. a system designed to offer hints may still give answers or make mistakes. a system grounded in course documents can cite materials and still require independent accuracy checks. [s01](07-source-records.md#s01) [s10](07-source-records.md#s10)\n\n## before a pilot\n\ndescribe the learners, setting, expected benefits, accessibility needs, human supports, data handling and acceptable alternatives. ask whether the same problem could be addressed by existing teaching, peers, books or an assistive tool. define how learners and educators can decline, report problems or stop. these steps are dux proposals to be adapted and evaluated.\n\n## during a pilot\n\nobserve whether the tool provides the help intended, whether learners can challenge or leave it, and whether errors reach students. invite feedback without making participation coercive. measure learning and accessibility as well as use and satisfaction. monitor outcomes across groups only with appropriate privacy safeguards and sufficient sample sizes.\n\n## after a pilot\n\ncompare results with the stated goal and an appropriate alternative, including a later assessment where feasible. consider the workload, cost, data risks and who was unable to participate. report missing outcomes and uncertainty. the nigerian intervention bundled ai access, teaching and extra time; its measured program effect cannot identify which component caused the result. [s09](07-source-records.md#s09)\n\nthe medical-school deployment observed usage and feedback, but did not systematically measure answer accuracy or establish improved learning. its own authors note constraints on interpretation. [s10](07-source-records.md#s10)\n", "number": "10"}, {"path": "11-learning-activities.html", "title": "Learning activities to try and evaluate", "kind": "synthesis", "topic": "literacy", "text": "# learning activities to try and evaluate\n\nthese activities are examples, not validated lesson plans. adapt them to learner age, disability, language, goals, resources and local policies. participation in an ai activity should not be the only route to the learning objective.\n\n## compare, check, explain\n\ngive a learner two short explanations of a topic, one generated and one from a suitable human-authored source. ask what is supported, what is missing, how they would check a claim and what remains uncertain. use examples appropriate to the learner; provide sources and human help. the aim is calibrated evaluation, not automatic distrust. the small short-text study shows that explaining limitations alone did not significantly change over-reliance on its task. [s07](07-source-records.md#s07)\n\n## try, seek a hint, return\n\ninvite an initial attempt, then a hint or conversation with a teacher, peer, book or tool. ask the learner to revisit the original problem and explain the decision. offer needed assistive supports. a later, accessible transfer task can help evaluate whether the activity supported learning. the turkish study motivates testing scaffold design, but does not prove this activity works. [s01](07-source-records.md#s01)\n\n## design an ai-free path\n\ninvite learners to choose a period for reading, making, discussing or practicing without a generative tool when that fits the objective. reflect afterward on what was easy, difficult or worth checking. this is an option, not a moral rule or an instruction to abandon accessibility support. evidence in this collection does not establish a universal time threshold.\n\n## for educators and families\n\nuse a fictitious example to discuss personal information, consent and the option to stop; identify a trusted person to ask for help. adults can review the tool and its rules in advance. these are safety-oriented suggestions informed by child-rights guidance, not evidence of their standalone effectiveness. [s05](07-source-records.md#s05)\n", "number": "11"}, {"path": "12-study-comparisons.html", "title": "Comparing three studies", "kind": "synthesis", "topic": "evidence", "text": "# comparing three studies\n\nthe studies below are useful because they do not deliver one simple answer. each evaluates a different package of support, population, comparison and outcome. none should be used as a ranking of people or a prediction for a particular learner.\n\n## high-school mathematics in türkiye\n\na classroom-assigned study compared textbook-and-notes practice with two gpt-4 designs, then used unassisted exams within each session. the general interface improved practice but reduced the subsequent exam score relative to control; the teacher-informed tutor largely mitigated the loss. one school and short-term outcomes constrain generalization. [s01](07-source-records.md#s01)\n\n## undergraduate physics in the united states\n\na crossover study compared a purpose-built ai tutor used at home with active learning in class. immediate post-test outcomes favored the tutor under the study conditions. the comparison changes mode and setting together, and does not establish durable effects. [s08](07-source-records.md#s08)\n\n## after-school learning in nigeria\n\na randomized, six-week program offered structured content, teacher support and ai to interested students, compared with regular instruction. the program improved measured scores in the reported analysis; endline attrition differed by group. the design does not separate ai from extra time and other support. [s09](07-source-records.md#s09)\n\n## what to ask next\n\ntest similar tools against equal-time alternatives, measure delayed learning and transfer, include accessible and diverse settings, report missing data, and examine possible harms. better task performance, user confidence, and learning are distinct outcomes. this is a research agenda, not a result already demonstrated.\n", "number": "12"}, {"path": "13-accessibility-and-participation.html", "title": "Accessibility and participation", "kind": "synthesis", "topic": "access", "text": "# accessibility and participation\n\naccessibility is part of the learning goal, not a final decoration. people may use screen readers, captions, alternative input, adjusted presentation, translated materials or assistive systems. the right combination depends on the individual and activity. a proposed ai tool should be assessed with disabled learners and the assistive technologies they actually use.\n\n## separate participation from a particular tool\n\noffer accessible ways to read, respond, ask questions and seek help. an ai-free option should not remove an accommodation someone needs; an ai-based option should not require a person to disclose disability or private information unnecessarily. these are dux design proposals, not evidence of effectiveness in every setting.\n\n## standards and human review\n\nw3c’s wcag 2.2 provides recommendations for accessible web content and notes that it does not address every need. automated tests can identify some issues but do not replace assessment with people and assistive technologies. a cited standard does not certify an application. [s14](07-source-records.md#s14)\n\n## equity beyond access\n\nask who can afford the tool, whether connectivity is reliable, whether language and examples fit the learner, and whether participation exposes personal data. unesco’s broader education-technology report asks whether a technology is relevant, equitable, scalable and sustainable. its assessment is not a test of a particular generative ai system. [s15](07-source-records.md#s15)\n", "number": "13"}, {"path": "14-governance-lifecycle.html", "title": "Governance across the life of a tool", "kind": "synthesis", "topic": "safety", "text": "# governance across the life of a tool\n\na decision to introduce ai in education does not end at procurement. institutions can define who is accountable, how risks and benefits will be assessed, when a tool will be reviewed, and how people can contest a harmful outcome. nist’s voluntary, general-purpose ai framework organizes its work around govern, map, measure and manage. it is not a school-specific validation or a checklist. [s13](07-source-records.md#s13)\n\n## govern and map\n\nidentify the decision owner, affected learners and educators, purpose, plausible harms and benefits, alternatives, data flows and limits of the proposed use. include people who will use or be affected by the service. map power as well as technology: who can decline, who can contest an outcome, and who carries the work of checking?\n\n## measure and respond\n\ndefine what counts as success and harm, the comparison, acceptable uncertainty and a route for incident reporting. check actual performance in context, including accessibility and output quality. set conditions for pausing, changing or retiring the tool. review when the model, provider, terms or population changes. these are dux proposals inspired by the framework, not proof of compliance with it.\n\n## protect agency\n\nhuman review requires someone with the authority, time and information to disagree with the tool. a nominal approval step without an ability to change the decision may not provide meaningful accountability. unesco’s teacher framework places human agency and responsibility at the centre of educators’ competencies. [s03](07-source-records.md#s03)\n", "number": "14"}, {"path": "15-questions-for-people.html", "title": "Questions for learners, families and educators", "kind": "synthesis", "topic": "literacy", "text": "# questions for learners, families and educators\n\nthere is no single right sequence for every learner. these prompts support conversation; they are not an assessment, a validated safety instrument, or a substitute for an educator’s or caregiver’s judgment.\n\n## for a learner\n\nwhat do i hope to understand? can i make a first attempt, alone or with the support i need? would a hint or a person help more than an answer? what evidence would make me change my mind? could i explain my conclusion? when would i choose to pause?\n\n## for a family\n\nwhat is age-appropriate for this child and this service? can we try an activity together? does the child know they can stop and speak to a trusted adult? what information does the service collect and how can we report a problem? unicef’s guidance centres children’s rights, safety and development, rather than offering a universal safe age. [s05](07-source-records.md#s05)\n\n## for an educator\n\nwhat learning objective is served? which students may be excluded? what will remain human-led? how will feedback and generated facts be checked? can students demonstrate their learning through an accessible alternative? how will the approach be evaluated and adjusted? unesco’s teacher framework treats agency and professional learning as core concerns. [s03](07-source-records.md#s03)\n\n## for an institution\n\nwho approves the service, reviews the agreement, manages data and responds when something goes wrong? what other approach could meet the same need? what evidence would cause a pause? are there adequate time and resources for educators to make the process meaningful? [s11](07-source-records.md#s11) [s13](07-source-records.md#s13)\n", "number": "15"}, {"path": "16-glossary.html", "title": "A glossary for the library", "kind": "synthesis", "topic": "evidence", "text": "# a glossary for the library\n\n## artificial intelligence\n\na broad term for systems designed to perform tasks associated with prediction, classification, generation or decision support. different systems use different techniques; a generative chatbot is one type, not the whole field.\n\n## generative ai and language models\n\ngenerative systems produce content such as text or images. a language model generates text from patterns learned during training. a fluent response is not itself evidence of factual accuracy, understanding or a reliable source. the design and capabilities of systems change over time.\n\n## ai literacy\n\nthe knowledge, skills and values needed to understand, question and engage with ai. unesco’s student framework includes human agency, ethics, techniques and design; it does not reduce literacy to prompting. [s02](07-source-records.md#s02)\n\n## scaffolding and feedback\n\nsupport that helps a learner work toward a goal, such as a question, hint, example or feedback. the term does not guarantee a benefit: the content and timing of support need evaluation. the two tutors in the turkish study used different prompts and teacher-provided materials. [s01](07-source-records.md#s01)\n\n## retrieval-augmented generation\n\na design that supplies retrieved material to a generative model when composing an answer. relevant source material may make answers easier to inspect, but retrieval does not guarantee correctness. the medical-school deployment did not systematically assess its response accuracy. [s10](07-source-records.md#s10)\n\n## outcome, comparator and unit\n\nan outcome is what a study measures, such as a later test score. a comparator is the alternative against which the intervention is assessed. the unit might be a person, classroom, student-session, conversation or model output. these must not be interchanged. [s01](07-source-records.md#s01) [s09](07-source-records.md#s09)\n\n## association and causal effect\n\nan association is a relationship observed in data; it need not mean one variable caused the other. randomized comparisons can support causal conclusions about the assigned intervention under their design and assumptions, but missing data, bundled interventions and limits to generalization remain important.\n", "number": "16"}, {"path": "17-teacher-work-and-agency.html", "title": "Teacher work, support and agency", "kind": "synthesis", "topic": "teaching", "text": "# teacher work, support and agency\n\nteachers decide what to teach, notice misunderstanding, build relationships, adapt materials and assess learning. tools may assist with particular tasks, but an output is not self-validating and time saved on one activity may be spent elsewhere. unesco’s teacher competency framework emphasises human agency, responsible use, pedagogy and continuing professional learning. [s03](07-source-records.md#s03)\n\n## time, quality and workload\n\na world bank data-catalogue description of a randomized program in peru reports increased adoption but no measured time savings on core teacher tasks. the program included training, a licence and follow-up; its full paper and outcome methods have not yet been examined here. treat this as a research lead, not a settled claim about all teachers or tools. [s16](07-source-records.md#s16)\n\nask whether a proposed use genuinely reduces workload after reviewing, correcting, documenting and explaining outputs. measure quality and equity alongside speed. consult teachers about what work they want supported and what decisions should remain theirs. these are proposed evaluation questions, not established effects.\n\n## preparing and reviewing materials\n\na teacher can use a system to generate alternatives or examples, then check facts, appropriateness, accessibility and representation against trusted materials. they can also choose a non-ai process. a human review step needs time and authority to reject or revise an output. keep personal student data out of a tool unless the specific use has been assessed and authorized. [s03](07-source-records.md#s03) [s11](07-source-records.md#s11)\n\n## professional learning\n\nconsider practical evaluation, privacy, bias, human accountability and pedagogical design, not only prompting. a framework can specify valuable competencies without proving a particular workshop achieves them. evaluate training over time with teachers and learners, and give people an accessible path to raise concerns.\n\n## further in the library\n\nfor survey figures on teachers’ ai use, guidance and self-estimated time savings, and why they differ from measured results, see [how ai is being used: what surveys show](28-what-surveys-show.md).\n", "number": "17"}, {"path": "18-community-and-belonging.html", "title": "Relationships, community and belonging", "kind": "synthesis", "topic": "children", "text": "# relationships, community and belonging\n\neducation happens between people as well as through materials. a tool may help a learner prepare questions or find an explanation, but educational quality also involves human connection, classroom participation and the ability to seek help. unesco’s broader technology report places learners’ interests and human interaction at the centre. [s15](07-source-records.md#s15)\n\n## what one new study asks\n\nan accepted 2026 paper reports a six-section randomized study in one chinese university of a combined preparation protocol involving ai, reflection, question generation and instructor responsiveness. it reports a section-level belonging difference, but the exact randomization tests and small number of sections warrant care. the abstract has been inspected, not the full methods; the intervention does not isolate the ai component. [s17](07-source-records.md#s17)\n\n## design for connection\n\nas a dux proposal, invite learners to bring unresolved questions to peers or a teacher, reserve time for discussion, and give people a way to ask a human for help. check whether the activity increases or reduces participation for different learners. provide alternatives for people who need different forms of communication.\n\n## children's relationships\n\nunicef has identified distinct child-rights concerns around conversational and companion-like systems. a tool should not be presented as a substitute for trusted people; family and institutional safeguards should be adapted to the child and setting. the guidance is not a measurement of prevalence. [s06](07-source-records.md#s06)\n", "number": "18"}, {"path": "19-comparing-support.html", "title": "Four studies of educational support", "kind": "synthesis", "topic": "teaching", "text": "# four studies of educational support\n\na study can test access to a tool, a tutor’s response to suggestions, a training package or a complete learning program. these interventions cannot be treated as interchangeable. each result depends on the comparison, participants, outcome and time frame.\n\n## a tutor supporting a student\n\nin the turkish mathematics experiment, the standard gpt-4 interface increased assisted practice performance but was followed by lower unassisted exam performance than notes and textbooks. the teacher-informed tutor avoided much of that loss but did not show an exam advantage over control. it was one school and a short-term assessment. [s01](07-source-records.md#s01)\n\n## a tool supporting a human tutor\n\nin the us tutor copilot preprint, tutors rather than children were randomized to access suggestions in live mathematics tutoring. the reported exit-ticket pass rate rose from 62% to 66%. tutor attrition, age-inappropriate suggestions, provider involvement and the short-term outcome matter. sessions and chat messages are not students. this does not test replacing human tutors. [s19](07-source-records.md#s19)\n\n## a program for teachers\n\nthe peru trial randomized school-level access to a package of training, a licence and follow-up. its working paper reports improved performance on certain ai-use assessments without measured time savings across seven self-reported tasks. student achievement was not measured as a demonstrated outcome. the package’s components cannot be separated by the main comparison. [s16](07-source-records.md#s16)\n\n## a structured tutor for adults\n\na small online mba preprint compared access to a course-grounded tutor with a holdout where consumer ai remained available. only 51 of 86 randomized students completed both researcher assessments; a separate instructor final was available for all 86. it reports a positive direction, but its setting and assessment choices prevent sweeping claims about adult learning. [s21](07-source-records.md#s21)\n\n## what can be compared\n\nfor each, ask: who was randomized, what changed together, what was measured, how many people completed the outcome, and how long did the effect last? a product demonstration, satisfaction survey, performance with assistance and unassisted retention each answer different questions. [method](06-method-and-questions.md)\n", "number": "19"}, {"path": "20-literacy-across-ages.html", "title": "Building AI literacy across ages", "kind": "synthesis", "topic": "literacy", "text": "# building ai literacy across ages\n\nai literacy is not confined to a single application. a person may encounter ai in a search result, a recommendation, a school tool or a workplace process without having chosen a chatbot. the oecd and european commission framework describes knowledge, skills and attitudes for primary and secondary learners, including critical evaluation and responsible use. it is a framework of goals, not an evaluation of a particular lesson. [s18](07-source-records.md#s18)\n\n## begin with a familiar decision\n\na younger learner might examine whether a generated description matches a picture and ask an adult for help. an older learner might compare source claims, discuss whose perspectives are missing, or examine a generated citation. an adult learner might evaluate a tool against professional responsibilities or compare explanations with a reliable reference. these are dux examples, not proven age bands or validated interventions.\n\n## practice judgment in both directions\n\nappropriate reliance involves noticing errors and recognizing when evidence supports a useful answer. the small estonian study found that one short text did not significantly reduce reliance on incorrect screenshots, while participants in the intervention rejected more correct answers. the study does not show that all ai literacy instruction fails. [s07](07-source-records.md#s07)\n\na university preprint randomized students to four forms of instruction about educational prompting and reports measured gains on a prompting rubric. only 431 of 979 randomized course enrollees remained in its complete-case analysis, and there was no direct between-condition difference on the final exam. prompt quality and general academic learning are different outcomes. [s20](07-source-records.md#s20)\n\n## questions for educators\n\nwhat does the learner already know? what can be demonstrated without requiring an account? how will the activity handle privacy and accessibility? can the learner use a non-ai route? what would count as a useful change in judgment after the lesson? learners should have a meaningful opportunity to disagree or stop. [s02](07-source-records.md#s02) [s05](07-source-records.md#s05)\n", "number": "20"}, {"path": "21-adult-learning-evidence.html", "title": "Evidence for adults and lifelong learning", "kind": "synthesis", "topic": "adult", "text": "# evidence for adults and lifelong learning\n\nadult learning includes formal university study, professional training, family learning and self-directed curiosity. these are different settings with different constraints. the current dux corpus contains several adult examples but does not represent all adult learners, occupations, languages or accessibility needs.\n\n## higher education\n\nthe harvard physics study compared a particular tutor at home with active learning in class across two topics and reported higher immediate post-test scores for the tutor condition. the complete-case requirements, one course and changed mode and environment limit generalization. [s08](07-source-records.md#s08)\n\nthe online mba preprint studied one finance course and a course-grounded tutor against access to ordinary resources, including consumer ai. its researcher assessment had substantial missingness; a separate instructor final covered all randomized students. it does not prove that voice or a particular interface generally produces deeper learning. [s21](07-source-records.md#s21)\n\n## professional learning\n\nin peru, a randomized package for sixth-grade teachers combined training, a licence and follow-up. the authors measured teacher ai-related skills and reported no time savings on seven core tasks. they did not establish an improvement in pupils’ learning. professional learning and student learning should not be conflated. [s16](07-source-records.md#s16)\n\na medical-school deployment studied usage and feedback, not a randomized learning outcome. some students used the tool for clarification, but the researchers did not systematically assess output accuracy and could not reconstruct individual use patterns from the conversation-level data. [s10](07-source-records.md#s10)\n\n## missing perspectives\n\nthere is limited primary evidence in this collection about older adult learners, foundational literacy programs, apprenticeships, disabled adult learners and communities with limited connectivity. involving these learners in design is a proposed practice; research on outcomes and unintended effects is still needed. [s14](07-source-records.md#s14) [s15](07-source-records.md#s15)\n", "number": "21"}, {"path": "22-reading-studies.html", "title": "Study design: reading claims carefully", "kind": "synthesis", "topic": "evidence", "text": "# study design: reading claims carefully\n\nevidence can be useful without answering every question. a careful reader can separate what a study observed, what its design supports, and what remains a proposal.\n\n## who and what was randomized?\n\na trial may assign people, tutors, classrooms or schools. participants in a cluster share conditions, so the number of learners is not automatically the number of independent randomized units. tutor copilot assigned tutors; the peru study assigned schools after network-level selection. the design and analysis must account for this structure. [s19](07-source-records.md#s19) [s16](07-source-records.md#s16)\n\n## how many outcomes were actually observed?\n\nenrollment, randomization and outcome completion can differ. the computer-science preprint randomized 979 course enrollees but analyzed 431 complete cases across all phases. a reduced sample may differ from those missing; it does not become 979 independent observations because that many were initially assigned. [s20](07-source-records.md#s20)\n\n## what was the alternative?\n\na program may bundle extra time, trained adults and technology. the nigeria study compares its combined after-school package with ordinary instruction; it cannot isolate the effect of the model from those other ingredients. a general tool, a teacher-informed tutor and a human tutor may all be meaningful comparators for different questions. [s09](07-source-records.md#s09)\n\n## what did the test actually measure?\n\nan exit ticket, an exam, a prompt-quality rubric, a self-report and a conversation count are not interchangeable. a short-term assessment can reveal an immediate difference without proving lasting retention or transfer. a finding that is not statistically significant is not proof that two approaches are equal. a source’s policy recommendation is not a measured intervention effect. [s01](07-source-records.md#s01) [s19](07-source-records.md#s19)\n\n## what was accessible?\n\nan abstract may omit recruitment, missing data or adverse outcomes; a dataset card is not necessarily the paper. access limitations in the [source records](07-source-records.md) describe the material dux actually inspected. an inaccessible supplement is an unknown, not evidence of an absence.\n\n## further in the library\n\ntwo chapters apply these questions to current claims: [effort, confidence and reliance](25-effort-confidence-reliance.md), on headlines about ai and the brain, and [how ai is being used: what surveys show](28-what-surveys-show.md), on survey figures.\n", "number": "22"}, {"path": "23-access-and-use.html", "title": "Implementation: access is not the same as use", "kind": "synthesis", "topic": "children", "text": "# implementation: access is not the same as use\n\nan education system may make a tool available without learners being able or willing to use it as intended. time, access, relationships, technical reliability, privacy, motivation and the fit between activity and learner can all affect implementation. usage is an outcome to examine, not proof that learning occurred.\n\n## two elementary-school trials\n\na 2026 working paper reports two randomized trials in which children used the same ai reading platform independently or with an in-person tutor supporting engagement. across the districts, 355 children were assigned; the programs differed in setting and tutor type. the human-support group spent slightly more time on the platform and read more stories, but overall usage remained low. exploratory reading scores did not significantly improve. the comparison does not tell us whether ai reading instruction beats a human tutor or no intervention. [s22](07-source-records.md#s22)\n\n## ask what happened in practice\n\na pilot can document the number offered access, who actually takes part, how often, what support is needed, and why people decline or stop. separate these observations from whether learning improved. participation data can themselves be sensitive, so collect only what is necessary and make reporting transparent. these are evaluation proposals, not measured outcomes of a dux pilot. [s11](07-source-records.md#s11) [s13](07-source-records.md#s13)\n\n## avoid a simple dose story\n\nthe provider of the studied platform offered a recommended usage level. that statement is a provider claim; the two trials do not establish a universal safe or effective number of minutes. increased screen time may displace another useful activity. compare the full learning experience, including human relationships and accessible alternatives, before deciding whether more use is desirable. [s22](07-source-records.md#s22)\n", "number": "23"}, {"path": "24-solutions-and-explanations.html", "title": "Solutions, explanations and hints", "kind": "synthesis", "topic": "independence", "text": "# solutions, explanations and hints\n\nseveral studies now compare learners who could ask an ai system for help with learners who could not, and then test them afterwards. read together, they suggest that the kind of help matters at least as much as whether help was available. the pattern is consistent enough to plan around, but most of the evidence for it is short-term, and part of it is exploratory.\n\n## with help and without it\n\nfour studies measured performance with support separately from performance afterwards, without it. in a turkish high school, open gpt-4 access raised practice scores but was followed by lower scores on an unassisted exam; a tutor built around teacher-provided hints largely avoided the drop. [s01](07-source-records.md#s01) in a lab study of english-as-a-second-language university students, chatgpt support improved essay revisions more than a human expert, a checklist or no support, but knowledge gain, transfer and motivation did not differ significantly. [s26](07-source-records.md#s26) in two lab experiments with novice programmers, chatbot access let students cover more practice questions without a significant change in their later post-test. [s23](07-source-records.md#s23) a product made with support is a different outcome from what a learner can do alone; studies, and classrooms, should measure both.\n\n## asking for answers or for understanding\n\nin the programming experiments, the researchers coded what students asked the chatbot. requests for explanations were associated with better post-test results at a given level of progress. when copy and paste was enabled, students asked for more complete solutions, and, at the same level of practice progress, those with access solved fewer post-test questions than their counterparts in the earlier study without copy and paste. these analyses were exploratory: the explanation requests were not randomized, and the copy-and-paste comparison spans two separate studies. [s23](07-source-records.md#s23) they fit the turkish result, where the guarded tutor steered students toward hints. [s01](07-source-records.md#s01)\n\n## when a worked example helps\n\nthe picture is not simply “solutions are bad”. in an online experiment with 274 adult crowdworkers, worked-solution hints written by chatgpt and checked by a person before use produced immediate gains on a short algebra or statistics test; the gains with human-authored hints were not significantly different. learners attempted each problem before seeing a hint, the test came minutes later, and about a third of generated hints had failed the human check and were replaced. [s27](07-source-records.md#s27) a purpose-built physics tutor with pre-written answers also produced strong immediate results. [s08](07-source-records.md#s08) worked examples after an attempt, checked for accuracy, are a different kind of help from a chatbot that completes the exercise.\n\n## what this suggests, and what it does not\n\nfor a learner: make an attempt first, ask for an explanation or a hint rather than the finished answer, and then try a similar problem without help. for an educator: decide which steps the tool may help with, and assess some learning without it. these are reasonable inferences from short studies, mostly with older students and adults. they are not proof that any one design works everywhere, and none of these studies measured long-term learning.\n", "number": "24"}, {"path": "25-effort-confidence-reliance.html", "title": "Effort, confidence and reliance", "kind": "synthesis", "topic": "independence", "text": "# effort, confidence and reliance\n\ngenerative ai can make a task feel easier. whether that lighter effort also means less learning or weaker judgment is one of the most discussed questions in this field, and one of the least settled. the sources here mostly measure self-reports, short tasks or brain activity, not long-term learning.\n\n## feeling that one has learned\n\nin two programming experiments, students with chatbot access rated their own learning higher than their measured post-test results justified. [s23](07-source-records.md#s23) in the turkish mathematics study, students’ perceptions also did not match their exam outcomes. [s01](07-source-records.md#s01) satisfaction and confidence are worth asking about, but they are not evidence that learning happened.\n\n## confidence in the tool and in oneself\n\na survey of 319 knowledge workers who used generative ai weekly described 936 workplace tasks. participants reported less effort for most thinking activities when using ai. higher confidence in the ai for a task was associated with less reported critical thinking; higher self-confidence went with more. the study is a one-time self-report survey of adults, so these are associations, not effects, and the authors note that some participants confused less effort using the tool with less thinking. [s25](07-source-records.md#s25) a small estonian study with 36 analysed students found that a short explanation of ai’s limits did not significantly reduce acceptance of wrong advice, and increased rejection of correct advice. [s07](07-source-records.md#s07) calibrated trust, not maximum suspicion, is the goal.\n\n## writing, memory and ownership\n\na widely shared mit media lab preprint recorded eeg while 54 adults wrote short essays with chatgpt, with web search or with no tool. the chatgpt group showed the weakest connectivity, and most of its members could not quote from the essay they had just written; they also reported lower ownership. the paper is under review, the sample is small, its optional fourth session involved 18 self-selected participants, and a published commentary questions its methods. eeg connectivity is not a measure of learning. [s24](07-source-records.md#s24) at most it supports a narrow point: in one short task, adults who handed the writing to a chatbot remembered less of what “they” wrote.\n\n## reading these claims\n\nheadlines about ai “rotting” or “boosting” the brain usually go further than the studies. ask: who took part? was anyone randomized? was learning measured, or a proxy such as effort, confidence or brain activity? was it measured later, without the tool? see [reading claims carefully](22-reading-studies.md). none of the studies here involves children, and none follows learners for more than a few months.\n", "number": "25"}, {"path": "26-tutors-in-lower-income-settings.html", "title": "AI tutoring in lower-income settings", "kind": "synthesis", "topic": "access", "text": "# ai tutoring in lower-income settings\n\nsome of the most encouraging results for ai in education come from programmes in low- and middle-income countries, where classes are large and extra tutoring is scarce. they deserve attention, and careful reading. in each case ai arrived as part of a supervised programme, not as an unsupervised app.\n\n## ghana: a maths tutor over whatsapp\n\nin schools run by one network in ghana, students used a maths chatbot on school-provided phones during two 30-minute study-hall sessions a week, with teachers present, for about eight months. eleven schools were randomized, five to the tutor. students who completed both tests grew 3.01 points more on a 35-item numeracy test than controls. the control group had no equal-time alternative, the small number of schools and student-level analysis may understate uncertainty, an author is affiliated with the organisation that built the tool, and two versions of the paper report different samples. [s28](07-source-records.md#s28)\n\n## nigeria: an after-school programme\n\nin benin city, interested senior secondary students were randomized to a six-week, teacher-supported after-school programme that combined materials, adults and gpt-4. the working paper reports gains in english and a combined assessment; attrition differed between groups, and the programme was compared with no extra sessions. [s09](07-source-records.md#s09)\n\n## peru: support for teachers\n\nin lima, teachers offered training, an ai assistant licence and follow-up scored higher on prompting and judged weak ai output better, without measured time savings. effects on students were not established. [s16](07-source-records.md#s16)\n\n## what these studies share\n\neach bundled ai with structure: set times, supervision, curriculum-linked content or training. none isolates the ai component from extra practice time. implementing organisations were involved in the research in at least two cases. together they suggest that supervised, curriculum-bound use can help in some settings; they should not be added together as independent proof of large effects, or used to argue that devices alone will close learning gaps. the unesco gem report’s questions about relevance, equity, scale and sustainability still apply. [s15](07-source-records.md#s15) access to a device is not the same as use, and use is not the same as learning. see [access and actual use](23-access-and-use.md).\n", "number": "26"}, {"path": "27-detectors-and-assessment-design.html", "title": "Detection, trust and assessment design", "kind": "synthesis", "topic": "assessment", "text": "# detection, trust and assessment design\n\nwhen students can produce fluent text with ai, it is tempting to look for a tool that can tell. the evidence collected here points the other way: detection scores are an unsafe basis for decisions about individual students, and the more durable response is to design assessments around what each task is meant to show.\n\n## what detectors got wrong\n\nin a 2023 benchmarking study, seven commercial and open detectors were run on 91 essays written for an english proficiency test by non-native speakers and 88 essays by us eighth-graders. on average the detectors labelled 61% of the non-native essays as ai-generated; at least one detector flagged 89 of the 91. the eighth-grade essays were classified almost perfectly. asking chatgpt to make the non-native essays sound more “native” cut the false-positive rate to about 12%, and asking it to simplify the eighth-grade essays raised theirs to about 57%. [s29](07-source-records.md#s29) detectors have changed since early 2023 and the samples were small, but the mechanism the authors identify, that plainer vocabulary looks “machine-like” to these tools, would tend to fall hardest on language learners and on writers who use simpler phrasing.\n\n## designing for what a task should show\n\nthe ai assessment scale describes levels of permitted ai use, from none to full, so that an educator can match permitted use to the learning outcome and tell students clearly. its 2025 revision moves further away from policing: it says tasks meant to be done without ai should happen in supervised settings rather than depending on detectors or honour codes, and that relabelling an unchanged task does not make it valid. [s30](07-source-records.md#s30) the scale is a design and communication framework; its developers note that its effect on learning or misconduct has not yet been established.\n\n## what students say\n\nin a 2025 pew survey, 59% of us teens said students at their school use ai chatbots to cheat at least somewhat often. that is a perception of peers, not a measured rate. [s32](07-source-records.md#s32) the same teens drew distinctions: in a 2024 pew survey, 54% thought using chatgpt to research a new topic was acceptable, while only 18% said the same of writing an essay. [s31](07-source-records.md#s31)\n\n## practical implications\n\n- do not use a detector score as the sole basis for an accusation. talk with the student and look at the work’s history.\n- say clearly, task by task, what kind of ai use is allowed and why.\n- when a skill must be shown without ai, assess it in a setting where that can be seen, with accessible alternatives.\n- where ai is allowed, ask learners to show their process: drafts, choices and what they checked.\n\nthese follow from the sources and from the library’s chapter on [assessment in an ai-rich setting](08-assessment.md). none of the sources shows that a particular redesign reduces misconduct or improves learning, and rules on ai in assessment differ between institutions.\n", "number": "27"}, {"path": "28-what-surveys-show.html", "title": "How AI is being used: what surveys show", "kind": "synthesis", "topic": "evidence", "text": "# how ai is being used: what surveys show\n\nsurveys tell us how many people say they use ai for learning and teaching, and what they think of it. they cannot tell us whether that use helps. the figures below come from different organisations, ages, questions and definitions, so they should be read side by side, not stitched into one trend.\n\n## teenagers in the united states\n\nin autumn 2024, 26% of us teens told pew they had ever used chatgpt for schoolwork, double the 2023 share; older teens and black and hispanic teens reported more use. [s31](07-source-records.md#s31) a year later, with a broader question about any ai chatbot, 54% said they had used one for schoolwork help, and 10% said they did all or most of their schoolwork with chatbot help. [s32](07-source-records.md#s32) the two questions differ, so the rise from 26% to 54% is not a measured trend.\n\n## younger children\n\na spring 2026 common sense media survey of 1,204 children aged 9 to 17 used a broad definition of ai that included search-result summaries. among the children who used ai, 85% said they used it for schoolwork, and 16% of those who used it for homework said they had difficulty starting or finishing homework without it. nearly three in four said their school or a teacher had talked about what ai use was allowed. [s34](07-source-records.md#s34) all of these are children’s own reports.\n\n## teachers\n\nin a spring 2025 gallup and walton family foundation survey of 2,232 us public school teachers, six in ten reported using ai for work. weekly users estimated saving 5.9 hours a week, a figure the report extrapolates to “six weeks a year”. only 19% said their school had an ai policy, and 68% had received no ai training. [s33](07-source-records.md#s33) the time figure is teachers’ own estimate. in a randomized programme for teachers in peru, measured time savings across seven tasks were not significant. [s16](07-source-records.md#s16) time savings are contested and measured in different ways.\n\n## reading survey figures\n\n- check who was asked, when, and exactly what the question said.\n- “ever used” is not “uses often”; “ai” may include search summaries; a named product is not every chatbot.\n- surveys that share a panel are not independent confirmation of each other.\n- self-reports of time saved, cheating by others or difficulty without ai are perceptions, not measurements.\n\nthese surveys are all from the united states. use elsewhere, and its effects on learning, are not established by them. see [reading claims carefully](22-reading-studies.md) and [access and actual use](23-access-and-use.md).\n", "number": "28"}, {"path": "source-s01.html", "title": "Learning performance and unassisted assessment", "kind": "source", "topic": "independence", "text": "# learning performance and unassisted assessment\n\n**source:** hamsa bastani, osbert bastani, alp sungu, haosen ge, özge kabakcı and rei mariman, “generative ai without guardrails can harm learning: evidence from high school mathematics,” *pnas* 122(26), e2422633122, 25 june 2025, [doi:10.1073/pnas.2422633122](https://www.pnas.org/doi/10.1073/pnas.2422633122). the [20 august 2025 correction](https://www.pnas.org/doi/10.1073/pnas.2518204122) was inspected: it corrects osbert bastani’s affiliation after a production error; it reports no changes to findings.\n\n**access and design:** publisher article and the relevant sections of the [author-hosted manuscript and appendix](https://hamsabastani.github.io/education_llm.pdf) inspected. the publisher-hosted supplement returned http 403; equivalence to the author-hosted version was not independently established. the preregistration itself was not inspected. preregistered classroom-assigned field experiment at one turkish high school in fall 2023, grades 9–11. four 90-minute sessions; approximately 50 classes and nearly 1,000 real students, not model-generated participants. the author-hosted appendix table 3 reports 839 students in the main survey sample: 277 gpt tutor, 320 control, and 242 gpt base; its survey sample including honors contains 943 students. the author version says the main sample excludes honors classes and nonrespondents to the baseline survey. these counts should not be described as total enrollment. the publisher main table 1 reports 2,848 student-session observations per outcome, not 2,848 people. exact enrollment and class counts remain unverified. unit of assignment: classroom; student-session performance analyzed, with classroom-clustered standard errors. honors classes excluded from main sample. students worked from teacher-provided problems, with notes/textbooks alone, a gpt-4 chat interface, or a teacher-informed gpt-4 tutor including solutions and hints, then took an unassisted exam.\n\n**finding:** relative to controls, practice scores were higher with both ai variants; the standard interface group scored 17% lower on the subsequent unassisted exam relative to controls. the guarded tutor group did not show a positive exam effect relative to controls, and the negative effect was largely mitigated. these are short-term results for this setting and these specific tools; they do not establish long-term outcomes, effects on all ages, or that every guardrail works. mechanism analyses suggest copying or reduced engagement, but do not definitively isolate the mechanism. see [publisher methods and results](https://www.pnas.org/doi/10.1073/pnas.2422633122).", "number": "01"}, {"path": "source-s02.html", "title": "Student AI competencies", "kind": "source", "topic": "literacy", "text": "# student ai competencies\n\n**source:** fengchun miao, kelly shiohira and natalie lao, unesco, *ai competency framework for students* (2024), [official publication page](https://www.unesco.org/en/articles/ai-competency-framework-students?hub=66682); [full framework, doi 10.54675/jkjb9835](https://doi.org/10.54675/jkjb9835).\n\n**access and design:** official overview and parts of framework pdf inspected. normative curriculum framework, not a trial or a participant sample. twelve competencies span human-centred mindset, ethics, ai techniques and applications, and system design, across understand, apply and create. it recommends agency and critical judgment. this specifies educational goals; it does not show that any particular curriculum causes improved learning.", "number": "02"}, {"path": "source-s03.html", "title": "Educator competencies", "kind": "source", "topic": "teaching", "text": "# educator competencies\n\n**source:** unesco, *ai competency framework for teachers* (2024), [official framework pdf](https://unesdoc.unesco.org/in/rest/annotationsvc/downloadwatermarkedattachment/attach_import_d418160e-3eb2-4d1c-8239-2744911cc721?_=391104eng.pdf&from=1&to=52).\n\n**access and design:** selected pdf sections inspected; normative professional-development framework, not a controlled outcome study. it emphasizes teacher agency, privacy, critical review, human interaction and learner control. its recommendation to test pedagogical strategies is a program-design principle, not proof of efficacy.", "number": "03"}, {"path": "source-s04.html", "title": "Age-appropriate implementation", "kind": "source", "topic": "children", "text": "# age-appropriate implementation\n\n**source:** unesco, *guidance for generative ai in education and research*, 7 september 2023, [official overview](https://www.unesco.org/en/articles/guidance-generative-ai-education-and-research?hub=67098).\n\n**access and design:** official overview inspected; full guidance not fully reviewed. policy and pedagogical guidance, not a trial. it calls for privacy protections, age limits for independent conversations, and age-appropriate validation and design. specific age thresholds and current jurisdiction-specific legal requirements have not been verified here; avoid presenting any recommendation as a universal law.", "number": "04"}, {"path": "source-s05.html", "title": "Children's rights and safeguards", "kind": "source", "topic": "children", "text": "# children's rights and safeguards\n\n**source:** unicef innocenti, *guidance on ai and children*, version 3.0, december 2025, [official report page](https://www.unicef.org/innocenti/reports/policy-guidance-ai-children).\n\n**access and design:** issuer's overview inspected; complete pdf not reviewed. rights-based guidance informed by consultation and existing evidence, including a twelve-country study; that number is countries, not a participant count. the overview lists safety, privacy, fairness, transparency, accountability, development, inclusion and children's ai skills among ten requirements. these are recommendations, not evidence that a particular safeguard has been validated or legal advice.", "number": "05"}, {"path": "source-s06.html", "title": "Conversational AI and children", "kind": "source", "topic": "children", "text": "# conversational ai and children\n\n**source:** unicef, *when ai becomes a friend: child rights risks, harms, and regulatory responses to ai chatbots and companions*, june 2026, [official document page](https://www.unicef.org/documents/when-ai-becomes-friend-child-rights-risks).\n\n**access and design:** issuer overview inspected, not full policy brief. policy analysis compares approaches across six jurisdictions as of may 2026; six is a jurisdiction count, not children or experiments. it identifies risks and recommends preventive safeguards and accountability. the overview does not quantify risk or establish prevalence.", "number": "06"}, {"path": "source-s07.html", "title": "A short literacy intervention", "kind": "source", "topic": "literacy", "text": "# a short literacy intervention\n\n**source:** brett puppart and jaan aru, *a brief ai literacy intervention does not significantly reduce over-reliance and increases under-reliance on chatgpt: a randomized study*, [arxiv:2503.10556v2](https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.10556v2), revised 18 september 2026; originally submitted 13 march 2025 under a different title. doi [10.48550/arxiv.2503.10556](https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2503.10556).\n\n**access and design:** selected methods, results and limitations of the 28-page [v2 pdf](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2503.10556v2) inspected, as well as the version history. preprint; peer-review or subsequent publication status not established. in october 2024 researchers invited 72 estonian schools; 10 forwarded invitations. forty-two real 12th-grade students completed the questionnaire; six were excluded for stated speed criteria, leaving 36 analyzed, ages 18–19: 19 intervention and 17 control. individuals were randomized. each saw eight selected math puzzles and fixed screenshots of model recommendations, four correct and four incorrect, and decided whether to adopt them; they did not interact live with the model. the control read a general introduction and the intervention received additional information. participant and trial are distinct units.\n\n**finding and limitations:** across participants, the reported mean proportion of incorrect recommendations adopted was 52.1%. the intervention group’s mean was 48.68% versus 55.88% for controls; the difference was not statistically significant (p = .471), which does not establish equivalence or no effect. rejection of correct advice was higher in the intervention group (36.84% versus 19.12%; p = .025). the small sample, selection, exclusion criteria, fixed screenshots and short task constrain precision and generalization; the study does not establish that ai literacy education generally fails, or what happens in ordinary iterative chatbot use.", "number": "07"}, {"path": "source-s08.html", "title": "Undergraduate physics tutor trial", "kind": "source", "topic": "adult", "text": "# undergraduate physics tutor trial\n\n**source:** greg kestin, kelly miller, anna klales, timothy milbourne and gregorio ponti, “ai tutoring outperforms in-class active learning: an rct introducing a novel research-based design in an authentic educational setting,” *scientific reports* 15, 17458, 3 june 2025. [doi:10.1038/s41598-025-97652-6](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-97652-6).\n\n**access and design:** publisher article methods and results inspected; supplement not inspected. two-week crossover randomized experiment in one harvard undergraduate introductory physics course, fall 2023. of 233 enrolled students, 194 met inclusion criteria involving consent, participation in both modes and completed tests. peer-instruction groups of 2–3 were assigned together. the study compared an ai tutor used at home with in-class active learning on two topics; its tutor used pre-written answers and content-specific prompting. the reported post-test counts are 142 ai-condition and 174 classroom-condition lesson observations, not distinct people.\n\n**finding and limits:** the paper reports higher immediate post-test performance in the tutor condition. the environments, delivery and conditions differ together; this does not isolate ai from all other differences, measure long-term retention, establish effects in other learners or show that ai generally replaces teachers.", "number": "08"}, {"path": "source-s09.html", "title": "Nigeria after-school pilot", "kind": "source", "topic": "teaching", "text": "# nigeria after-school pilot\n\n**source:** martin elias de simone, federico hernan tiberti, maria barron rodriguez, federico manolio, wuraola mosuro and eliot jolomi dikoru, *from chalkboards to chatbots: evaluating the impact of generative ai on learning outcomes in nigeria*, world bank education global department working paper, may 2025. [working paper pdf](https://thedocs.worldbank.org/en/doc/0c08ef70ff0e1d81997f116923c0e2c7-0140022025/related/edu-wp-18-evaluating-the-impact-of-generative-ai-on-learning-outcomes-in-nigeria.pdf); [reproducibility package doi](https://doi.org/10.60572/cre7-j348).\n\n**access and design:** working paper methods and results and reproducibility-package overview inspected; data and code not independently rerun. interested first-year senior secondary students in benin city, nigeria, were individually randomized: 657 to a six-week teacher-supported after-school ai program and 671 to usual schooling. the endline assessment included 422 treatment and 337 control students, 759 total; these are people, not sessions. the program combined materials, adult support and gpt-4-based copilot. the control did not receive an equal-time alternative.\n\n**finding and limits:** the authors report a 0.31 standard-deviation improvement in a combined english, ai-knowledge and digital-skills assessment and 0.238 standard deviations in english; the relevant regression reports 654 observations, fewer than the 759 endline completers. differential attrition was significant; the paper reports sensitivity analyses, but selection remains a limitation. the bundled intervention does not establish the separate effect of ai, nor its long-term impact. the working paper’s projected earnings are projections, not observed participant outcomes.", "number": "09"}, {"path": "source-s10.html", "title": "Medical education observational study", "kind": "source", "topic": "adult", "text": "# medical education observational study\n\n**source:** thomas thesen and soo hwan park, “a generative ai teaching assistant for personalized learning in medical education,” *npj digital medicine* 8, 627, 4 november 2025. [doi:10.1038/s41746-025-02022-1](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41746-025-02022-1).\n\n**access and design:** publisher methods, selected results and limitations inspected. observational deployment of a course-material-grounded assistant across two medical-school cohorts, not a randomized learning-outcome trial. among survey respondents, 22 of 56 in the first cohort and 23 of 87 in the second reported at least one use. the paper reports 328 conversations in thematic analysis (216 / 65.9% in its first topic category); conversations are not distinct participants, and topic codes can overlap. gpt-4o assisted thematic coding; a 15% subset received human validation. the authors did not systematically assess answer accuracy or track individual engagement patterns. findings describe usage and reported experience in this setting; they do not establish learning gains or that retrieval eliminates hallucination.", "number": "10"}, {"path": "source-s11.html", "title": "Evaluating education-technology privacy", "kind": "source", "topic": "safety", "text": "# evaluating education-technology privacy\n\n**source:** u.s. department of education, student privacy policy office, [privacy and education technology](https://studentprivacy.ed.gov/privacy-and-education-technology), webpage, publication date not established; accessed 12 november 2026.\n\n**access and design:** agency overview inspected, linked model terms and underlying legal guidance not individually reviewed. the page links resources on assessing how online services collect, use and transmit information. this is a us agency resource, not a study or a complete statement of requirements in every jurisdiction. laws and interpretations can change.", "number": "11"}, {"path": "source-s12.html", "title": "Child privacy and schools", "kind": "source", "topic": "safety", "text": "# child privacy and schools\n\n**source:** u.s. federal trade commission, [complying with coppa: frequently asked questions](https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance/resources/complying-coppa-frequently-asked-questions), section n, webpage accessed 12 november 2026.\n\n**access and design:** selected agency faq on schools inspected; not a trial. it describes limited circumstances in which a school may act for a parent in relation to educational services, and distinguishes school-benefit uses from other commercial purposes. the detailed requirements and applicable law depend on context and may change. dux does not provide legal advice or assert that this us guidance applies elsewhere.", "number": "12"}, {"path": "source-s13.html", "title": "AI risk management framework", "kind": "source", "topic": "safety", "text": "# ai risk management framework\n\n**source:** elham tabassi, national institute of standards and technology, *artificial intelligence risk management framework (ai rmf 1.0)*, 26 january 2023, [doi:10.6028/nist.ai.100-1](https://doi.org/10.6028/nist.ai.100-1); [core functions](https://airc.nist.gov/airmf-resources/airmf/5-sec-core/).\n\n**access and design:** nist publication overview and online core inspected. voluntary, non-sector-specific governance framework, not a school efficacy study or a binding universal standard. it organizes activities around govern, map, measure and manage, and says the functions are not a checklist. nist notes a revision is in progress; check current status before use.", "number": "13"}, {"path": "source-s14.html", "title": "Web accessibility guidance", "kind": "source", "topic": "access", "text": "# web accessibility guidance\n\n**source:** world wide web consortium, *web content accessibility guidelines 2.2*, [w3c recommendation, 12 december 2024](https://www.w3.org/tr/2024/rec-wcag22-20241212/).\n\n**access and design:** recommendation overview and publication history inspected. technical accessibility standard, not an empirical evaluation of an ai tutor. w3c states its guidelines do not address every need. conformance of any dux or third-party service has not been established by inclusion here.", "number": "14"}, {"path": "source-s15.html", "title": "Education technology in context", "kind": "source", "topic": "access", "text": "# education technology in context\n\n**source:** unesco global education monitoring report team, *technology in education: a tool on whose terms?*, 2023, [official publication page](https://www.unesco.org/gem-report/en/publication/technology?hub=83250).\n\n**access and design:** issuer’s overview and selected summary text inspected, not every underlying study. the report considers education technology through relevance, equity, scalability and sustainability. its broader education-technology scope must not be recast as a trial of generative ai. it advocates learning centred on people and attention to potential adverse consequences.", "number": "15"}, {"path": "source-s16.html", "title": "Teacher AI program in Peru", "kind": "source", "topic": "teaching", "text": "# teacher ai program in peru\n\n**source:** carla z. glave, carolina lopez, ezequiel molina and román andrés zárate, *not faster, but better: generative ai and teacher skill in peru 2025*, world bank dataset description, 20 july 2026, [doi:10.48529/t6x6-2y70](https://doi.org/10.48529/t6x6-2y70).\n\n**access and design:** primary data catalogue description and abstract inspected; full paper, dataset and preregistration not inspected. the record describes 1,269 sixth-grade teachers across 390 public schools in metropolitan lima, with school-network eligibility and school-level randomization. it identifies a program combining training, a copilot licence and follow-up, with further randomized message features. the catalogue abstract reports greater adoption without measured time savings on core tasks; this is a bundled program claim, not evidence on student learning or on ai software in isolation. the catalogue is a research lead: outcome measures, attrition, uncertainty and paper version require full-paper verification before stronger claims.\n\n**update, 12 november 2026:** the [october 2026 working paper](https://documents.worldbank.org/curated/en/099309210012625906/pdf/idu-c261aadb-6c32-48af-a07c-9cff5e86c716.pdf), policy research working paper 11476, doi [10.1596/1813-9450-11476](https://doi.org/10.1596/1813-9450-11476), is now accessible; selected methods, results and tables were inspected. of 390 schools, 196 (660 teachers) were assigned the package and 194 (609 teachers) control. controls retained access to publicly available ai. the authors report higher scores on prompt rubrics and better judgments of weak ai output, and no significant time reduction across seven self-reported tasks. time analysis table 3 lists 607 observations; other analyses have different denominators. the authors report no differential endline response, which does not remove all selection concerns. student learning was not established. dataset and code were not rerun; the research was funded in part by the microsoft ai economy institute. the earlier catalogue-only access note is retained as the record of the initial review.", "number": "16"}, {"path": "source-s17.html", "title": "Undergraduate preparation and belonging", "kind": "source", "topic": "adult", "text": "# undergraduate preparation and belonging\n\n**source:** renlin wang, jian sun and shu wen, “structured generative ai preparation and classroom belonging in a six-class randomized trial,” *scientific reports*, accepted article published online 26 october 2026, [doi:10.1038/s41598-026-78039-7](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-026-78039-7).\n\n**access and design:** publisher abstract inspected; complete methods, data and supplement not independently reviewed. the publisher labels it an accepted version subject to further editing. six intact classes at one chinese university were randomized, three sections and 110 students per condition. the intervention combines ai exploration, confidence reflection, question generation and instructor responsiveness. it measures classroom belonging and related reported outcomes; the section is the assignment unit, and the number of sections is six, not 220 independent randomized units.\n\n**finding and limits:** the abstract reports a difference in belonging at week 12 under its section-level analysis, while its exact two-sided randomization tests give p = .100 for the primary contrasts. the complex intervention does not isolate ai or establish effects in other settings. this is a limited-access research lead pending full methods review, not a broad claim that ai improves belonging.", "number": "17"}, {"path": "source-s18.html", "title": "OECD and European Commission AI literacy framework", "kind": "source", "topic": "literacy", "text": "# oecd and european commission ai literacy framework\n\n**source:** oecd and european commission, *empowering learners for the age of ai: an ai literacy framework for primary and secondary education*, 18 june 2026, [doi:10.1787/65cd27d4-en](https://doi.org/10.1787/65cd27d4-en).\n\n**access and design:** official overview and abstract inspected; complete 64-page framework not fully reviewed. normative framework for desired learning outcomes; no participant count or intervention effect. it includes understanding, critical evaluation and ethical and creative use. it complements, rather than experimentally validates, unesco’s framework. specific activities and age progression require review of the full report.", "number": "18"}, {"path": "source-s19.html", "title": "AI support for human tutors", "kind": "source", "topic": "teaching", "text": "# ai support for human tutors\n\n**source:** rose e. wang, ana t. ribeiro, carly d. robinson, susanna loeb and dora demszky, *tutor copilot: a human-ai approach for scaling real-time expertise*, [arxiv:2410.03017](https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.03017), 3 october 2024 version inspected. peer-review and subsequent version status not established.\n\n**access and design:** preprint methods and selected results inspected; preregistration and data not independently checked. two-month study in a southern us district, nine schools and grades 3–8. the district identified 1,787 students; 900 tutors were initially randomized, 450 per arm; at study launch 782 remained, 386 treatment and 396 control. the text also reports 874 full-time tutors assigned to schools, a separate figure whose relationship to the launch sample is not fully clarified here. randomization was by tutor, not student. final analysis comprises 4,136 sessions and over 550,000 chat messages, neither being a count of individuals. intervention tutors could request ai-generated suggestions; control tutors taught without it. the lead author worked part-time with the provider.\n\n**finding and limits:** the paper reports exit-ticket pass rate of 62% control versus 66% treatment, a four percentage-point intention-to-treat difference. it does not establish lasting achievement, effects in other settings, or safety of unsupervised child-facing chatbots. tutors reported some suggestions were not grade appropriate. classifier-based analysis of chat language and tutor interviews are different kinds of evidence from the exit-ticket outcome.\n\n**update, 12 november 2026:** a later [november 2025 working-paper version](https://edworkingpapers.com/sites/default/files/ai24_1054_v2.pdf) was located and selected methods and limitations inspected. it reports 900 tutors initially assigned; 783 with study sessions (388 treatment, 395 control), 1,534 students initially eligible and 1,013 attending at least one study session, in grades 3–6. these differ from the 2024 version’s figures above; do not merge versions or silently substitute counts. in the newer version students could be matched with different tutors across sessions. it reports the same direction for the session-level exit-ticket outcome; the authors report no statistically significant end-of-year math effect and caution that their tutor-randomized design cannot directly estimate a student-level treatment effect on that outcome. the later version’s different sample definitions and coding figures require version-specific citation.", "number": "19"}, {"path": "source-s20.html", "title": "Prompting instruction in introductory computer science", "kind": "source", "topic": "literacy", "text": "# prompting instruction in introductory computer science\n\n**source:** ruiwei xiao, runlong ye, xinying hou, jessica wen, harsh kumar, michael liut and john stamper, *transforming genai policy to prompting instruction: an rct of scalable prompting interventions in a cs1 course*, [arxiv:2602.16033](https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.16033), 17 february 2026 version inspected. preprint; peer-review or later version not established.\n\n**access and design:** preprint methods and selected results inspected. at one north american university, 979 enrolled students were randomized to four instruction conditions; 431 with valid responses across all phases formed the complete-case sample (102, 112, 109 and 108 respectively). this 44% complete-case rate limits interpretation. students generated prompts assessed against the researchers’ rubric; the most active condition included gpt-4o feedback. the authors report gains in the measured prompting task and no direct final-exam differences between conditions. the relationship between prompting gains and exam scores is observational within the study and does not establish that prompting practice caused higher course performance.", "number": "20"}, {"path": "source-s21.html", "title": "Structured tutoring and modality in an online MBA", "kind": "source", "topic": "adult", "text": "# structured tutoring and modality in an online mba\n\n**source:** shihao yang, marshall van alstyne and chrysanthos dellarocas, *when ai tutors speak: evidence from a randomized field experiment*, [arxiv:2609.23958](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.23958), working draft dated 20 september 2026. preprint; peer review not established.\n\n**access and design:** methods and selected results of the draft inspected. in one online mba finance course, 86 consenting students were randomized to a structured course-grounded tutor or a holdout that could use consumer ai. fifty-one completed both study assessment waves; the paper also reports instructor gradebook outcomes for all 86. within the tutor arm, modality alternated weekly. the paper reports 32 students in the within-student voice/text comparison. students, assessment waves, weekly interactions and course examinations are distinct units.\n\n**finding and limits:** the researchers report an advantage for tutor access on their assessment and a smaller same-direction difference on the instructor’s final. attrition affects the custom-assessment analysis; the final was not itself a preregistered outcome. no general conclusion about all adults or all voice tutors follows, and a non-significant modality difference does not by itself prove equivalence. the tutor, subject, cohort and short field period bound the findings.", "number": "21"}, {"path": "source-s22.html", "title": "Human engagement support for elementary reading", "kind": "source", "topic": "children", "text": "# human engagement support for elementary reading\n\n**source:** carly d. robinson, david gormley, ana trindade ribeiro and susanna loeb, *access is not enough: human support improves engagement with ai tutoring*, edworkingpaper 26-1451, june 2026, [doi:10.26300/pz7p-p388](https://doi.org/10.26300/pz7p-p388); [author-hosted paper](https://scale.stanford.edu/sites/default/files/ai26-1451.pdf).\n\n**access and design:** working-paper methods and selected results inspected; preregistration and underlying data not independently checked. two individually randomized trials compared an ai reading platform with the same platform plus in-person engagement support. district a assigned 84 treatment and 90 control students in grades 1–5; district b assigned 36 treatment and 145 control students in grades 1–3, 355 children total. the human role focused on engagement and troubleshooting, not direct reading instruction; scheduled intervention structures differed. the study is not a comparison of ai tutoring with human instruction or no tutoring.\n\n**finding and limits:** the paper reports roughly one additional minute per week of platform use in district a and 4.4 in district b, with low usage overall. exploratory reading achievement outcomes were not significantly different; this does not prove no effect. districts, measures and implementation varied, and the provider’s recommended usage level is a provider claim, not an established universal threshold.", "number": "22"}, {"path": "source-s23.html", "title": "How students use a chatbot while learning to code", "kind": "source", "topic": "independence", "text": "# how students use a chatbot while learning to code\n\n**source:** matthias lehmann, philipp b. cornelius and fabian j. sting, *ai meets the classroom: when do large language models harm learning?*, [arxiv:2409.09047v2](https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.09047v2), revised 8 march 2025. it was first submitted on 29 august 2024 as [v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.09047v1) under the title \"ai meets the classroom: when does chatgpt harm learning?\" doi [10.48550/arxiv.2409.09047](https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2409.09047). crossref also lists an ssrn version under the v1 title, [doi:10.2139/ssrn.4941259](https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.4941259). the ssrn page refused the connection and was not inspected. preprint or working paper. as of 12 november 2026, no journal publication was found and peer-review status is not established.\n\n**access and design:** the arxiv version history and the selected methods, results, exploratory analyses and limitations of the 38-page v2 pdf were inspected. the pre-registrations (aspredicted links in the paper) and any data were not inspected. the paper reports three studies:\n\n- **study 1** is an observational field study of two graduate python courses at a public dutch university in spring 2023. it covers 56 and 57 students, and the analysis unit is the student–question: 6,775 possible observations, 6,594 analysed after unanswered questions were excluded. students cannot be seen using chatgpt. use is estimated from how similar each submission is to 50 regenerated chatgpt (gpt-3.5-turbo-0613) solutions, and chatgpt outages serve as an instrument.\n- **study 2** is a pre-registered, incentivized laboratory experiment at a public german university in june 2024. individual students were randomized to have or not have a chatgpt (gpt-3.5-turbo-0125) chat window during a 45-minute python learning phase. pre-test and post-test each had 20 questions in 20 minutes, with no ai access. 108 were recruited and 107 analysed after one pre-registered exclusion. most had no python experience (79%) or were beginners (17%). the lab computers blocked copy and paste, which the authors say was unintended.\n- **study 3** repeated study 2 three weeks later with copy and paste enabled: 72 enrolled, 69 analysed.\n\nexploratory analyses pool studies 2 and 3: 176 participants, 94 of them with chatbot access. they hand-code the messages participants sent as requests for solutions or for explanations. studies, students, student–questions and chatbot messages are distinct units.\n\n**finding:** neither experiment found a statistically significant effect of chatbot access on post-test learning. in study 2, adjusted p = .580. in study 3, adjusted p = .298. in study 3, access increased the number of practice questions completed during the learning phase (p = .013). the exploratory analyses report the following:\n\n- **copy and paste:** with copy and paste enabled, participants asked for more solutions but not more explanations. holding learning-phase progress constant, study 3's treated participants solved about 1.4 fewer post-test questions than study 2's.\n- **explanations:** asking for explanations was associated with higher post-test performance at a given level of progress (p = .05).\n- **prior knowledge:** participants with lower pre-test scores benefited less than those with higher scores.\n- **perceived learning:** chatbot access raised self-rated learning beyond what measured learning explains.\n\nthese findings do not establish that substitution causes poorer understanding in general:\n\n- the copy-and-paste contrast compares two sequential studies, not randomized arms within one study.\n- requests for explanations were not randomized.\n- the prior-knowledge split is a median split on the pre-test.\n\nthe field result rests on a similarity proxy for ai use in two courses at one institution. sessions were short, participants were mostly novice university students, and the model is from 2024. effects on school-age learners, other subjects, other tools or longer-term retention are not established.", "number": "23"}, {"path": "source-s24.html", "title": "EEG study of essay writing with ChatGPT, search or no tool", "kind": "source", "topic": "independence", "text": "# eeg study of essay writing with chatgpt, search or no tool\n\n**source:** nataliya kosmyna, eugene hauptmann, ye tong yuan, jessica situ, xian-hao liao, ashly vivian beresnitzky, iris braunstein and pattie maes, *your brain on chatgpt: accumulation of cognitive debt when using an ai assistant for essay writing task*, [arxiv:2506.08872v2](https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.08872v2), revised 31 december 2025. the original v1 was submitted on 10 june 2025. doi [10.48550/arxiv.2506.08872](https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2506.08872). the pdf footer reads \"preprint, under review.\" as of 12 november 2026, no peer-reviewed publication was found, and peer-review status is not established. a [comment by miloš stanković, ella hirche, sarah kollatzsch and julia nadine doetsch, arxiv:2601.00856](https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.00856) (29 december 2025, itself a preprint) was also located. only its abstract was inspected.\n\n**access and design:** the arxiv version history was inspected, along with selected sections of the 216-page v2 pdf: abstract, summary table, participants, protocol, session 1 and session 4 interview results, the eeg statistics description and limitations. the full eeg, nlp and appendix analyses were not reviewed. data and code were not inspected. it was not established what changed between v1 and v2.\n\n- **sample:** 60 adults were recruited and 55 completed at least three sessions. 54 are reported, 18 per group, from five universities in the boston area. the paper reports ages 18–39 with m = 22.9 and sd = 1.69.\n- **assignment:** participants were randomly assigned to three groups, balanced for age and gender: chatgpt only, web search with ai answers suppressed, or no tools. each wrote a 20-minute essay on sat-style prompts in each of three sessions over about four months while wearing a 32-channel eeg headset.\n- **session 4:** this optional session had 18 participants who chose to return. nine from the original chatgpt group wrote without tools and nine from the original no-tool group used chatgpt. each chose from topics they had already written on. the search group had no session 4.\n- **measures:** eeg connectivity (ddtf, with rmanova and fdr correction as described), nlp analysis of the essays, scoring by two english teachers and an ai judge, and interviews after each session. the interviews asked participants to quote their own essay.\n- **units:** participants, sessions, essays and electrode-pair connections are distinct units.\n\n**finding:**\n\n- **connectivity:** the authors report the weakest eeg connectivity in the chatgpt group, intermediate in the search group and strongest in the no-tool group.\n- **quoting:** in session 1, 15 of 18 chatgpt-group participants could not provide a quote from the essay they had just written, compared with 2 of 18 in each other group. none of the 18 chatgpt-group participants gave a correct quote.\n- **ownership:** chatgpt-group participants reported lower ownership of their essays.\n\n\"cognitive debt\" is the authors' interpretive framing. the study does not measure learning or retention beyond in-session recall and quoting, and eeg connectivity is not itself a learning outcome. the sample is small and drawn from nearby institutions. session 4 is a self-selected subsample of 18, with nine per arm, writing on familiar topics. the task is a short timed essay with one chatbot. it does not establish long-term cognitive harm, effects on children, or effects of other ways of using ai. the commentary raises concerns about sample size, reproducibility, eeg methods, reporting inconsistencies and transparency. whether the authors addressed these in v2 is not established.", "number": "24"}, {"path": "source-s25.html", "title": "Knowledge workers' self-reported critical thinking with generative AI", "kind": "source", "topic": "adult", "text": "# knowledge workers' self-reported critical thinking with generative ai\n\n**source:** hao-ping (hank) lee, advait sarkar, lev tankelevitch, ian drosos, sean rintel, richard banks and nicholas wilson, \"the impact of generative ai on critical thinking: self-reported reductions in cognitive effort and confidence effects from a survey of knowledge workers,\" in *proceedings of the 2025 chi conference on human factors in computing systems* (chi '25, yokohama, 26 april–1 may 2025), [doi:10.1145/3706598.3713778](https://doi.org/10.1145/3706598.3713778). crossref lists online publication on 25 april 2025, pages 1–22, cc by 4.0. [author-hosted pdf](https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/wp-content/uploads/2025/01/lee_2025_ai_critical_thinking_survey.pdf) on the [microsoft research publication page](https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/publication/the-impact-of-generative-ai-on-critical-thinking-self-reported-reductions-in-cognitive-effort-and-confidence-effects-from-a-survey-of-knowledge-workers/).\n\n**access and design:** crossref metadata, the microsoft research page and the methods, results table, discussion and limitations of the 23-page author-hosted pdf were inspected. the pdf metadata is dated january 2025. the acm digital library page returned a bot-check page and was not inspected, so equivalence of the author pdf to the acm version of record is not established. chi is a peer-reviewed venue, but the review process for this paper was not separately verified. six of the seven authors list microsoft research affiliations and the first lists carnegie mellon university.\n\n- **design:** a cross-sectional online survey on prolific of adults who reported using generative ai at work at least once a week.\n- **sample:** 333 responses were received and 14 excluded for low quality, leaving 319 participants. of these, 159 were men and 153 women, 44.83% were aged 25–34, and the top countries of residence were the uk (37), canada (25) and the us (20).\n- **examples:** participants described 957 workplace examples, of which 936 were retained after 22 were removed. examples are not people.\n- **measures:** for each example, a yes/no item on whether critical thinking was enacted, five-point ratings of effort change for six bloom's-taxonomy activities, and confidence items.\n- **analysis:** mixed-effects regressions with a random intercept per participant and benjamini–hochberg correction across 98 predictors. model ns range from 753 to 930 examples because participants could answer n/a.\n\n**finding:** participants reported enacting critical thinking in 555 of 936 examples (59.29%). for each cognitive activity, most examples were rated \"less\" or \"much less\" effort with generative ai: 72% for knowledge, 79% comprehension, 69% application, 72% analysis, 76% synthesis and 55% evaluation. higher task-specific confidence in the ai was associated with lower reported enaction of critical thinking (p < .001) and with lower reported effort for five of six activities. higher self-confidence was associated with higher reported enaction (p = .026). qualitative coding describes a shift toward verifying, integrating and overseeing ai output.\n\nall measures are self-reports in a single survey, so the associations are not causal estimates of ai's \"impact.\" the authors note that participants sometimes conflated reduced effort in using the tool with reduced critical thinking. the sample is english-speaking, skews younger, and uses ai frequently. the study does not measure actual critical-thinking performance and does not cover students or children.", "number": "25"}, {"path": "source-s26.html", "title": "Chatbot, human expert, checklist or no support for essay revision", "kind": "source", "topic": "independence", "text": "# chatbot, human expert, checklist or no support for essay revision\n\n**source:** yizhou fan, luzhen tang, huixiao le, kejie shen, shufang tan, yueying zhao, yuan shen, xinyu li and dragan gašević, \"beware of metacognitive laziness: effects of generative artificial intelligence on learning motivation, processes, and performance,\" *british journal of educational technology* 56(2), 489–530. crossref lists online publication on 10 december 2024 and the print issue as march 2025. [doi:10.1111/bjet.13544](https://doi.org/10.1111/bjet.13544). also on [arxiv:2412.09315v1](https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.09315), submitted 12 december 2024, whose record links the journal doi.\n\n**access and design:** crossref metadata and the arxiv record were inspected, along with the methods, results, discussion and limitations of the 43-page arxiv pdf. the wiley article page returned a bot-check page and was not inspected, so equivalence of the arxiv text to the published version is not established. crossref lists no correction. this is a journal article; the peer-review details were not separately verified. the appendix and trace data were not reviewed.\n\n- **sample:** 117 university students (mean age 22.61, 70% identifying as female, 55% undergraduates) took part in a lab session between july and september 2023. all used english as a second language. the first author's affiliation is peking university. the inspected text's methods do not name the country, and the participants' first language is redacted as \"[disclosed].\"\n- **assignment:** individuals were randomized to no extra support (30), chatgpt 4.0 (35), a human writing expert (25) or writing-analytics checklist tools (27).\n- **chatbot conditions:** the chatgpt condition ran through an api, was restricted to the task topics and was limited to advice rather than generating the essay. whether one or several human experts provided support was not verified here.\n- **task:** a two-hour reading and writing task, a one-hour revision with the assigned support, and a post-test within one day.\n- **measures:** intrinsic motivation; self-regulated learning processes from logged clicks, keystrokes and page views; essay-score improvement; knowledge gain on a repeated knowledge test; and a transfer test.\n\n**finding:** essay-score improvement differed across groups (f = 4.549, p = .005). the chatgpt group improved by about two points more on the essay score than each other group (mean differences 1.97–2.20), with adjusted p from .012 to .037. knowledge-test post-scores did not differ significantly (p = .438), nor did transfer-test scores (p = .996) or intrinsic motivation. process mining showed fewer metacognitive process transitions in the chatgpt group, such as orientation and evaluation, than in the human-expert and checklist groups.\n\n\"metacognitive laziness\" is the authors' interpretation of these process patterns. they state that targeted, mature measures of it were lacking. essay-score gains measure a revised product made with support, not unassisted learning. the single task, short duration, modest group sizes and gender imbalance limit generalization. non-significant differences do not establish equivalence or absence of an effect, and long-term effects were not measured.", "number": "26"}, {"path": "source-s27.html", "title": "ChatGPT-generated versus human-authored math hints", "kind": "source", "topic": "evidence", "text": "# chatgpt-generated versus human-authored math hints\n\n**source:** zachary a. pardos and shreya bhandari, \"chatgpt-generated help produces learning gains equivalent to human tutor-authored help on mathematics skills,\" *plos one* 19(5), e0304013, published 24 may 2024 (received 15 august 2023, accepted 3 may 2024). [doi:10.1371/journal.pone.0304013](https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0304013). participant data: [doi:10.6084/m9.figshare.23935269](https://doi.org/10.6084/m9.figshare.23935269). an earlier pilot is the authors' preprint *learning gain differences between chatgpt and human tutor generated algebra hints*, [arxiv:2302.06871](https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.06871), submitted 14 february 2023.\n\n**access and design:** the 18-page publisher pdf was inspected, covering methods, results, discussion and limitations. for the pilot, only the arxiv abstract page was inspected. the published peer-review history, the figshare data and the supplementary files were not inspected. the article is peer-reviewed.\n\n- **design:** an online 3 × 4 experiment. each participant was randomized to no hints, human tutor-authored hints or chatgpt hints, and separately to one of four topics: elementary, intermediate or college algebra, or statistics.\n- **participants:** amazon mechanical turk workers with \"master\" status and at least a high-school degree. they were adults, not school students, and their median age group was 36–40.\n- **recruitment and exclusions:** 394 were recruited and 120 excluded for not attempting or not completing their assigned topic. that left 274: 90 control, 86 human hints and 98 chatgpt hints.\n- **procedure:** a three-item pre-test, five practice problems with the assigned help, and a post-test repeating the same three items. payment was $8 for an expected 10–20 minutes.\n- **hints:** chatgpt 3.5 (the \"dec 15 version\") generated worked-solution hints. the second author checked them for a correct answer, correct work and appropriate language. problems whose hints failed were replaced, so participants saw only hints that passed. on average 24 of the problems prompted (32%) failed this check.\n- **units:** people, problems and hints are distinct units.\n\n**finding:** mean pre-to-post gains were 17.01 percentage points with chatgpt hints (pre 43.51%, post 60.52%), 11.62 with human hints and 1.85 for controls. in post-hoc tests, chatgpt hints differed significantly from control (p = .011). human hints did not differ significantly from control (p = .087), and chatgpt and human hints did not differ significantly from each other (p = .416). the authors report that pre-tests were even within each topic.\n\nthe title's \"equivalent\" rests on a non-significant difference. no formal equivalence test was found in the sections inspected, so equivalence is not established. hints were quality-screened by a person before learners saw them, so the result does not show that unchecked chatbot output is safe or effective. further limits are the three-item measure, an immediate post-test, crowdworker adults, high attrition (about 30%) and a text-only 2023 model. the authors' earlier pilot (n = 77) reported the opposite direction, favouring human hints. do not merge the two versions' findings.", "number": "27"}, {"path": "source-s28.html", "title": "WhatsApp math tutor in Ghanaian schools", "kind": "source", "topic": "access", "text": "# whatsapp math tutor in ghanaian schools\n\n**source:** owen henkel, hannah horne-robinson, nessie kozhakhmetova and amanda lee, *effective and scalable math support: evidence on the impact of an ai-tutor on math achievement in ghana*, [arxiv:2402.09809](https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.09809). v1 was posted 15 february 2024 and v2 on 5 may 2024. doi [10.48550/arxiv.2402.09809](https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2402.09809).\n\n- the v2 pdf carries a different title: \"effective and scalable math support: experimental evidence on the impact of an ai-math tutor in ghana.\"\n- crossref lists a chapter with that title in *artificial intelligence in education: posters and late breaking results…* (communications in computer and information science, springer, published online 2 july 2024, pp. 373–381), [doi:10.1007/978-3-031-64315-6_34](https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-031-64315-6_34). the chapter itself was not inspected because springer refused the connection. its relationship to v2 and its review process are not established.\n- a follow-up trial is registered as [aea rct registry 14476](https://doi.org/10.1257/rct.14476). only its crossref record was seen, and no results were found.\n\n**access and design:** the arxiv version history, the arxiv abstract page and the full v1 (9-page) and v2 (10-page) pdfs were inspected. the versions report different samples, and the arxiv abstract page still uses v1's figures:\n\n- **students described:** v1 and the arxiv abstract page say “approximately 1,000” in grades 3–9; the v2 pdf says “approximately 500”.\n- **baseline sample:** v1 reports 850, including 213 ninth-graders absent at endline; v2 reports 637 in grades 3–8 (336 treatment, 301 control).\n- **completed both tests:** v1 reports 478; v2 reports 477 (241 control, 236 treatment; its table 1 lists 237).\n- **effect size:** 0.37 on the abstract page, 0.36 in the v1 text, and 0.36 in v2.\n\ncite counts by version and do not substitute between them.\n\n- **assignment:** schools were randomized. 11 rising academies schools were assigned, 6 to control and 5 to treatment, so the effective number of assigned units is small.\n- **intervention:** for about 8 months of 2023, treatment students used rori on school-provided phones in two 30-minute study-hall sessions a week, with teachers present, in addition to regular classes.\n- **comparison:** the control had no equal-time alternative.\n- **assessment:** the same 35-item grade 3–5 numeracy and algebra assessment at baseline (february) and endline (august).\n- **analysis:** the inspected versions report student-level t-tests on growth scores. they do not describe adjustment for clustering by school.\n- **usage data and affiliations:** v1 reports that individual attendance and usage were not tracked in detail. one author lists an affiliation with rising academies, which developed rori.\n\n**finding:** mean growth was 5.13 points for treatment and 2.12 for control, a difference of 3.01 points on the 35-item test, reported as p < .001 and d = 0.36. students who completed both tests had higher baseline scores than those who did not (22.26 versus 18.53). ceiling effects affected older students.\n\nthis is a first-year result for a supervised in-school supplement, not unsupervised home use. because there was no equal-time control, extra practice time cannot be separated from ai features. the small number of randomized schools, the possible understatement of uncertainty from student-level tests, the attrition and the version inconsistencies limit confidence. the study does not establish effects on the grade-level curriculum, lasting gains, effects outside this school network, or the \"extra year of learning\" equivalence that the authors suggest.", "number": "28"}, {"path": "source-s29.html", "title": "AI-text detectors and non-native English writers", "kind": "source", "topic": "assessment", "text": "# ai-text detectors and non-native english writers\n\n**source:** weixin liang, mert yuksekgonul, yining mao, eric wu and james zou, “gpt detectors are biased against non-native english writers,” *patterns* 4(7), 100779, july 2023, [doi:10.1016/j.patter.2023.100779](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.patter.2023.100779). author-posted version: [arxiv:2304.02819v3](https://arxiv.org/abs/2304.02819v3), 10 july 2023. earlier versions are v1 (6 april 2023) and v2 (18 april 2023).\n\n**access and design:** the arxiv abstract page, version history and full 9-page v3 pdf were inspected. the v3 abstract points to the published *patterns* article. the publisher's full text and pdf returned http 403, so the published version was not checked and its match to v3 is not established. crossref metadata for the doi was inspected. it gives volume 4, issue 7, article number 100779, issue date july 2023, publisher elsevier, and a cc by-nc-nd 4.0 licence for the version of record. it lists no correction or update. that is not proof that none exists. the publisher's article-type label (for example, research article or opinion) was not inspected. the study is a small benchmarking study of text, with no human participants. its units are essays and other texts, not writers:\n- **main comparison:** seven off-the-shelf detectors, accessed 15 march 2023, were run on 91 human-written toefl essays and 88 us eighth-grade essays. the toefl essays (year ≤ 2020) came from a chinese educational forum. the eighth-grade essays came from the hewlett foundation asap dataset.\n- **detectors:** originality.ai, quil.org, sapling, the openai detector hosted on hugging face, crossplag, gptzero and zerogpt.\n- **rewriting experiments:** the researchers used chatgpt to rewrite essays, with the “march 14 version of chatgpt 3.5” stated in the methods. the discussion section refers to a “gpt-4 intervention” for the toefl rewrite. this inconsistency is not resolved in v3.\n- **bypass experiments:** 31 usable chatgpt-3.5 college-admission essays were generated, from 70 attempts across 7 common app prompts. a separate set used 145 stanford cs224n project titles.\n- **statistics:** perplexity was measured with gpt-2 xl. comparisons used one-sided paired t-tests.\n- **code and data:** stated to be on github and zenodo. neither was rerun here.\n- **limitations stated by the authors:** small samples; most detectors used gpt-2 backbones; and only perplexity-based and supervised detectors were covered.\n\n**finding:** for the toefl essays, v3 reports an average false-positive rate of 61.22% across the seven detectors. all seven flagged 18 of 91 essays (19.78%) as ai-generated, and at least one detector flagged 89 of 91 (97.80%). the detectors were described as near-perfect on the eighth-grade essays.\n- **rewriting toefl essays:** prompting chatgpt to “enhance the word choices to sound more like that of a native speaker” cut the average false-positive rate from 61.22% to 11.77%.\n- **simplifying eighth-grade essays:** “simplifying” the word choices in the eighth-grade essays raised their average misclassification from 5.19% to 56.65%.\n- **self-editing generated text:** asking chatgpt to self-edit its own text lowered detection rates. for essays, rates fell from up to 100% to up to 13%. for abstracts, they fell from up to 68% to up to 28%.\n- **authors' explanation and caution:** they attribute the pattern to lower text perplexity. they “strongly caution” against using gpt detectors in evaluative or educational settings.\n\nwhat this does not establish:\n- that current detectors, which have changed since march 2023, show the same rates;\n- results for other languages, ages or proficiency levels;\n- that the two essay sets differ only in writers' language background (task, prompt, age and source also differ);\n- what false-positive rate any particular school would see in practice.\n\nit is evidence that detector scores alone are an unsafe basis for misconduct decisions. it is not a validated estimate of how often detectors err in current classroom use.", "number": "29"}, {"path": "source-s30.html", "title": "The AI Assessment Scale and its revision", "kind": "source", "topic": "assessment", "text": "# the ai assessment scale and its revision\n\n**source:** mike perkins, leon furze, jasper roe and jason macvaugh, “the artificial intelligence assessment scale (aias): a framework for ethical integration of generative ai in educational assessment,” *journal of university teaching and learning practice* 21(06), 2024, [doi:10.53761/q3azde36](https://doi.org/10.53761/q3azde36); [journal page](https://open-publishing.org/journals/index.php/jutlp/article/view/810). revised version: mike perkins, jasper roe and leon furze, “reimagining the artificial intelligence assessment scale: a refined framework for educational assessment,” *journal of university teaching and learning practice* 22(7), 2025, [doi:10.53761/rrm4y757](https://doi.org/10.53761/rrm4y757); [journal page](https://open-publishing.org/journals/index.php/jutlp/article/view/1707).\n\n**access and design:** for both articles the journal landing page, full pdf and crossref metadata were inspected. the two are conceptual and normative frameworks. they report no participants, no trial and no outcome data.\n- **date and licence discrepancies, original article:** the pdf gives received 4 december 2023, revised 16 february 2024, accepted 13 march 2024 and published 30 april 2024, under cc by-nc-sa 4.0. the landing page gives received 14 april 2024 and published online 19 april 2024, under cc by-nd 4.0. crossref gives 19 april 2024. these do not agree.\n- **revised article dates:** the pdf gives submitted 15 april 2025, revised 12 august 2025, accepted 25 september 2025 and online first 27 september 2025. the landing page and crossref give published online 3 december 2025.\n- **peer review:** both carry journal editorial details and received or accepted dates. the review process itself was not inspected.\n- **crossref metadata error:** crossref swaps the given and family names of the revision's second author, and the journal's generated apa citation repeats the error (“jasper, r.”). the pdf's own citation reads “perkins, m., roe, j., & furze, l. (2025)”.\n- **declarations in the revision:** no conflicts of interest and no external funding. the authors say they used chatgpt (o3-mini-high) and claude 3.5 sonnet for ideation, some draft text and editing.\n- **earlier preprint:** an [arxiv preprint, 2412.09029](https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.09029), titled “the ai assessment scale revisited,” exists. it was not inspected and should not be cited in place of the journal version.\n\n**finding:** the original aias sets out five levels of permitted generative-ai use in an assessment, from “no ai” to “full ai”. it is meant to let educators match permitted use to learning outcomes and to make expectations clear. its own limitations section says the scale “has only recently been put into operation in a pilot cohort” and that “there is no empirical research on the scale’s effectiveness in combating academic misconduct or improving student outcomes.”\n\nthe 2025 revision keeps five levels: no ai, ai planning, ai collaboration, full ai and ai exploration. its main changes are these:\n- it replaces the traffic-light colours with a non-hierarchical design;\n- it drops the old level 3 requirement to append unassisted original work, because detection cannot verify such submissions;\n- it says level 1 should take place in supervised settings, not rely on honour systems or detectors;\n- it presents the scale as a tool for redesigning assessments, not a label. it warns that relabelling an unchanged task “level 3” does not improve validity.\n\nit sets the aias alongside the “two-lane” model and accepts critiques that rules alone are “discursive” and need structural redesign. the revision says the scale has been implemented in “hundreds of institutions” and translated into 30 languages. these are the developers' own claims. it cites a pilot (furze et al., 2024) as early evidence, which was not inspected, and says “further empirical validation is crucial.”\n\nthe aias is a design and communication framework. its effect on learning, misconduct or assessment validity has not been established by these papers, and the authors developed the scale they recommend.", "number": "30"}, {"path": "source-s31.html", "title": "US teens' use of ChatGPT for schoolwork, fall 2024", "kind": "source", "topic": "children", "text": "# us teens' use of chatgpt for schoolwork, fall 2024\n\n**source:** olivia sidoti, eugenie park and jeffrey gottfried, pew research center, “about a quarter of u.s. teens have used chatgpt for schoolwork – double the share in 2023,” short read, 15 january 2025, [official page](https://www.pewresearch.org/short-reads/2025/01/15/about-a-quarter-of-us-teens-have-used-chatgpt-for-schoolwork-double-the-share-in-2023/); [methodology pdf](https://www.pewresearch.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/20/2025/01/sr_25.01.15_teens-chatgpt_methodology.pdf); [topline pdf](https://www.pewresearch.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/20/2025/01/sr_25.01.15_teens-chatgpt_topline.pdf).\n\n**access and design:** the short read, methodology and topline were inspected. this is a cross-sectional, self-administered web survey of 1,391 parent–teen dyads, with us teens aged 13–17, fielded 18 september to 10 october 2024.\n- **mode and panel:** run by ipsos in english and spanish on knowledgepanel, a probability-based panel recruited mainly by address-based sampling. teens were reached through their parents.\n- **response:** 3,233 panelists were invited and 1,391 qualifying dyads completed the survey. the completion rate was 51.5% and the cumulative response rate 2.7%.\n- **weighting and error:** results are weighted to us teens aged 13–17 who live with parents. the margin of error is ±3.3 percentage points for all teens. it is larger for subgroups, for example ±8.0 points for black teens (unweighted n = 247), because the survey oversampled black and hispanic respondents.\n- **ethics review:** an external irb (advarra) approved the study.\n- **question asked:** “have you ever used chatgpt to help with your schoolwork?” it went only to teens who had heard of chatgpt (unweighted n = 1,056) and was then re-based on all teens. it measures any use ever, of one named product. it does not measure how often teens use it, any other chatbots, or whether use was permitted.\n- **trend comparison:** the 2023 figures come from a survey fielded 26 september to 23 october 2023, not inspected here.\n- **measurement limits:** all measures are teen self-reports.\n\n**finding:** 26% of all us teens said they had ever used chatgpt to help with schoolwork, up from 13% in 2023. that is 34% of teens who had heard of chatgpt. the short read reports:\n- black and hispanic teens: 31% each, compared with 22% of white teens.\n- grades 11–12: 31%, compared with 20% in grades 7–8.\n- gender and household income: no difference in use.\n- acceptability, among all teens: 54% said using chatgpt to research new topics is acceptable. for solving math problems the figure was 29% (28% not acceptable), and for writing essays 18% (42% not acceptable).\n- awareness: 79% had heard of chatgpt.\n\nthese are population estimates of self-reported use. they do not show how intensively teens used it, what they produced, whether the use was misconduct, or any effect on learning. the figure is specific to chatgpt and should not be equated with use of ai chatbots in general (see s32).", "number": "31"}, {"path": "source-s32.html", "title": "How US teens use AI chatbots, including for schoolwork, fall 2025", "kind": "source", "topic": "children", "text": "# how us teens use ai chatbots, including for schoolwork, fall 2025\n\n**source:** colleen mcclain, monica anderson, olivia sidoti and william bishop, pew research center, *how teens use and view ai*, 24 february 2026, [official report page](https://www.pewresearch.org/internet/2026/02/24/how-teens-use-and-view-ai/); [report pdf](https://www.pewresearch.org/wp-content/uploads/sites/20/2026/02/pi_2026.02.24_teens-and-ai_report.pdf).\n\n**access and design:** the report page and full pdf, including methodology and topline, were inspected. a related demographic page and an earlier pew short read on teens' chatbot use were not inspected. this is a cross-sectional, self-administered web survey of 1,458 parent–teen dyads, with us teens aged 13–17, fielded 25 september to 9 october 2025.\n- **mode and panel:** ipsos knowledgepanel, in english and spanish.\n- **response:** 3,516 panelists were invited, 2,067 were eligible and 1,458 completed. the study-level response rate (aapor rr1) was 45% and the cumulative response rate 1.2%.\n- **error and ethics review:** the margin of error is ±3.3 percentage points for teens and for parents. an external irb (advarra) approved the study.\n- **question wording:** items ask about “artificial intelligence (ai) chatbots like chatgpt, copilot or character.ai”. they are not the chatgpt-specific items in s31, and the report presents no trend comparison with them.\n- **bases:** the detailed schoolwork items were asked of teens who had used chatbots for schoolwork help (unweighted n = 766). the cheating-perception item was asked of non-homeschooled teens who had heard of chatbots (unweighted n = 1,256). results are also re-based on all teens.\n- **measurement limits:** all measures are self-report. the cheating item records what teens perceive about other students.\n\n**finding:** among all teens:\n- 64% said they use ai chatbots.\n- 54% said they had ever used chatbots to get help with schoolwork.\n- 10% said they do all or almost all (3%) or most (7%) of their schoolwork with chatbot help. another 21% said some and 23% said a little.\n- for specific school tasks: 48% had used chatbots to research a topic, 43% to solve a math problem and 35% to edit something they wrote.\n- 26% rated chatbots extremely or very helpful for schoolwork.\n- 59% said students at their school use ai chatbots to cheat at least somewhat often, 14% rarely or never, and 15% were not sure. the figure was 76% among teens who had used chatbots for schoolwork.\n\nthe cheating figure is a perception of peers, not a measured rate of misconduct. these figures should not be read as a straight rise from s31's 26%: the product, wording and question flow all differ. the report does not measure learning outcomes, whether use was permitted, or the quality of help received.", "number": "32"}, {"path": "source-s33.html", "title": "US teachers' AI use and self-estimated time savings", "kind": "source", "topic": "teaching", "text": "# us teachers' ai use and self-estimated time savings\n\n**source:** walton family foundation and gallup, *teaching for tomorrow: unlocking six weeks a year with ai*, june 2025, [gallup-hosted report pdf](https://www.gallup.com/file/analytics/691922/walton-family-foundation-gallup-teachers-ai-report.pdf); andrea malek ash, gallup, “three in 10 teachers use ai weekly, saving six weeks a year,” 24 june 2025, [official article](https://news.gallup.com/poll/691967/three-teachers-weekly.aspx).\n\n**access and design:** the gallup article and full report pdf were inspected. the gallup-hosted and [walton-hosted](https://static.waltonfamilyfoundation.org/df/fb/eba12807470a9402d7433cc47dba/teaching-for-tomorrow-unlocking-six-weeks-a-year-with-ai-report.pdf) copies have identical extracted text. the survey ran on the web from 18 march to 11 april 2025, with 2,232 us public k-12 teachers recruited from the rand american teacher panel, a probability-based panel.\n- **weighting and error:** the sample was weighted to national teacher and school characteristics. the margin of error is ±2.5 percentage points for all teachers. subgroup unweighted counts, including weekly ai users, are not reported in the inspected text.\n- **peer review:** this is a sponsored survey report, not peer-reviewed. the walton family foundation commissioned it.\n- **how the time figure was built:** teachers who used ai for a task at least monthly said whether it saved time, made no difference or added time. those who reported a change estimated the hours per week, to the nearest half hour. net estimates per task were trimmed at the 2.5th and 97.5th percentiles, then summed across six non-overlapping tasks: worksheets, assessments, modifying materials, grading or feedback, analysing student data, and administrative work.\n- **inconsistency in the text:** the methodology defines the “ai dividend” as an average among teachers using ai at least monthly. the 5.9-hour figure is reported for teachers using ai at least weekly.\n- **measurement limits:** this is self-estimated time. there is no time-use diary, observation or comparison group.\n\n**finding:** six in ten teachers reported using an ai tool for work in 2024–25: 32% at least weekly, 28% less often and 40% not at all.\n- **time saved by task:** among teachers using ai for a given task, 60% to 84% said it saved time on that task, and 7% or fewer said it took more time.\n- **the headline figure:** teachers using ai at least weekly estimated saving 5.9 hours per week on average. weekly users estimated 5.9 hours and monthly users 2.9 hours.\n- **“six weeks a year”:** gallup multiplies 5.9 hours by 37.4 contracted weeks to get 220.66 hours, then divides by a 37.5-hour contracted week. this is an extrapolation of a self-estimated weekly figure, not measured annual time.\n- **policy and training:** only 19% of teachers said their school has an ai policy. teachers at such schools were more likely to have used ai (70% vs 60%). the report gives 2.3 vs 1.7 hours saved per week per teacher by policy status, but the inspected text does not explain the basis of that per-teacher average. it is a cross-sectional association, not evidence that policies cause savings. 68% had received no school or district ai training.\n- **quality:** majorities of task users reported higher-quality work, from 57% for grading and feedback to 74% for administrative work.\n\nthe survey does not establish actual time saved, what the time was used for (the reinvestment examples are qualitative quotes), effects on students, or the accuracy of ai-assisted grading.\n\n**update, 12 november 2026:** two later gallup releases were inspected. they use different samples and measures and should not be merged with the 2025 figures.\n- **guidance, 2026:** andrea malek ash and katherine senseman, “[most teachers receive no formal guidance on ai use](https://news.gallup.com/poll/710534/teachers-receive-no-formal-guidance.aspx),” 26 may 2026. this is from the walton–gallup study *teaching for tomorrow: closing the expectations gap*, a web survey of 2,069 us public k-12 teachers from the rand american teacher panel, fielded 9 february to 2 march 2026, margin of error ±2.5 points. it reports that 18% of teachers receive any formal administrator guidance on ai use for their work. across 10 tasks, 34% receive no guidance and 48% only informal guidance. the full report was not inspected.\n- **time for what matters, 2026:** megan brenan, “[teachers using ai say it frees up time for what matters most](https://news.gallup.com/poll/715364/teachers-using-say-frees-time-matters-most.aspx),” 14 october 2026. this surveyed 1,952 us k-12 “educators” from the probability-based gallup panel, fielded 28 july to 14 august 2026, margin of error ±2.7 points. unlike the earlier sample, it includes paraprofessionals, substitutes and other classroom staff, not only teachers. 59% said they used ai tools for work in 2025–26. among those users, 13% said ai gave them a lot more time for the parts of the job that matter most, 51% somewhat more, 29% no effect, and 7% said it cost them time. this is a different question from the 2025 hours estimate.", "number": "33"}, {"path": "source-s34.html", "title": "US children's AI use, ages 9–17, spring 2026", "kind": "source", "topic": "children", "text": "# us children's ai use, ages 9–17, spring 2026\n\n**source:** eleanor o’neil, kristen soltis anderson, baxter white, laura zimmermann, supreet mann and michael b. robb, *the common sense media census: ai use by tweens and teens, 2026*, common sense media, 2026, [report pdf](https://www.commonsensemedia.org/sites/default/files/research/report/2026-ai-use-by-tweens-and-teens.pdf). the exact release date was not established.\n\n**access and design:** the full 60-page pdf, including methodology and supplemental tables, was inspected. the report's landing page was not reachable. this is a cross-sectional, self-administered web survey of 1,204 us children aged 9–17, fielded 18–26 march 2026 by ssrs.\n- **panel and language:** children were reached through parents in the ssrs opinion panel, which is recruited from an address-based sample plus a prepaid-cellphone rdd sample. 1,154 children answered in english and 50 in spanish.\n- **response:** 1,421 were invited. the completion rate was 84.73% and the cumulative response rate 4.01%.\n- **weighting and error:** results are weighted to us children aged 9–17. the margin of error is ±3.8 percentage points.\n- **questionnaire:** developed with echelon insights, whose staff are co-authors, and pretested with six cognitive interviews. the median completion time was about seven minutes.\n- **funding:** the report names foundation and philanthropic funders. it was not peer-reviewed.\n- **what counted as “ai”:** the survey's definition was broad and included ai summaries in search results.\n- **measurement limits:** all measures are self-report by children, including some as young as 9.\n\n**finding:**\n- **overall use:** 86% of children said they use or interact with any ai, and 24% daily. this includes search summaries, which 75% had encountered. 67% had used ai chatbots.\n- **schoolwork, among ai users (n = 1,032):** 85% use ai for help with schoolwork or homework. 48% do so at least weekly, including 21% daily.\n- **homework difficulty:** 16% of homework users said they have difficulty starting or completing homework without ai.\n- **devices, among ai users:** 44% use ai on a school computer or tablet and 78% on their own device.\n- **school communication, all children:** 73% said their school or a teacher had communicated this school year about what they should and should not use ai for in schoolwork. 56% said the school had discussed using ai safely, and 51% had heard how to tell whether ai information is accurate.\n- **wellbeing:** heavier users more often reported loneliness. the report itself says a single survey “cannot determine” the direction of this association or rule out other factors.\n\nthe census does not measure learning, whether use was permitted, or the accuracy of what children received. because of the broad definition, “uses ai” is not the same as “uses a chatbot”.", "number": "34"}];
